window.harnessChunks["rows-40d290cc17f40e99.js"]=["<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T03:08:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ffffff233/DeepSeekFathom\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DeepSeekFathom</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ffffff233/DeepSeekFathom/tree/2c68eff7bb7ae2eabf4d36a0331386f06af2d356\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2c68eff7bb7ae2eabf4d36a0331386f06af2d356\">2c68eff &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute uncertain coding tasks through model-selected file/shell/patch/search tools while reacting to actual outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code structure, tool failures, unavailable files, incomplete edits, provider output and intermediate execution results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model selects the next tool, whether to delegate, repair after an error, or finalize in response to returned observations. - Decision owner: native model-directed FathomAgent together with the first-party tool-action/feedback loop; inference provider does not own the app&#39;s tool selection control wiring. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry, turn/session records, context compaction, approvals, thinking/todo mode, hooks and result normalization. - Closure path: initial user task → model tool decision → first-party tool or ephemeral child executes → actual result appended to session → next model decision or final answer. - Boundary reachability: standard installed CLI/desktop constructs FathomAgent with its local registered tools; this loop is the shipped entry-point, not a benchmark fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model&#39;s context-sensitive selection, tools, user approvals, session storage and a deterministic parser cannot decide situational coding actions. - Evidence: agent.py; cli.py; tools.py; tests. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high for source reachability; provider-backed execution not reproduced independently. - Caveats: approved actions are bounded by real permission modes and the operator&#39;s configuration.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party regulatory relation for specific interference or oscillation between multiple independent S1 operations was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: delegated tasks may address different subtasks, but current implementation returns each child&#39;s final summary in a parent-managed sequence with no shared conflict-feedback response. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root model picks delegates and task descriptions, runtime creates bounded children. No autonomous S2 conflict intervention or returned peer agreement. - Decision owner: coding parent S1 for delegation; code for sequencing. No distinct coordination owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child specs, bounded permission modes, sequential child invocation and tagged event/summary handling. - Closure path: child outcome → parent task transcript; no detected inter-S1 interference → attenuating correction → subsequent changed peer behavior loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task division, routing and ordered execution do not suffice for S2 under Methodology 0.3.7. - Evidence: delegate agent implementation; README; tool contracts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: user-supplied extensions may add coordination, but that configured external logic is not attributed to the first-party shipped mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-current authority owns portfolio priorities, current commitments, resourcing or accountabilities across operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current task subtasks, todo items and permission modes are local present operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root model manages its own coding approach; external user approves gated actions, chooses mode, and stops sessions. No whole-system managerial decision is returned to a set of sustained S1 units. - Decision owner: coding operative and human operator, not S3 manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task todos, child delegation, process tool/status, permission constraints, storage and hooks. - Closure path: tool/todo/child result to same coding model, not separate whole-current status → S3 discretionary intervention → changed worker commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent planning its current work is a function within S1; it does not become S3 because it spawns helpers. - Evidence: agent loop; policy; CLI. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: local .air development scheduler records are explicitly outside packaged runtime ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party complementary auditor with sufficiently independent evidence access, judgment and returned corrective path to S3 is wired into normal coding execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model-generated changes can be reviewed with diff/snapshot tools, optional delegated reviewer instructions or external hooks, but this is not an independent native whole-system audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operating model can inspect diff and decide whether to correct; user/developer may inspect history. No built-in separate auditor owns the audit verdict and current-control return. - Decision owner: coding operative for optional review, developer for external CI/hook behavior, not a credited S3 agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ReviewStore, git snapshot/diff, delegated child text, hook event callbacks and tests. - Closure path: diff/read/test output → operator or same model coding loop; no independent direct-evidence audit judgment → managerial owner → binding correction to ongoing workers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: review is a permission posture; .air/local/evidence/T2-review.md is development-only; a diff tool is evidence plumbing rather than independent audit closure. - Evidence: review service; agent delegation; hooks; review tests; README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported standard CLI. - Caveats: custom independently commissioned auditors could be composed externally, but are not first-party default ownership evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no integrated outward/future strategic adaptation loop changes the continuing agent organization&#39;s capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider aliases, current web search, skill inventory, plugins and updater are execution and human-managed configuration surfaces. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user selects model, permission mode, plugins/skills and optional update; the coding agent reacts to current task, not a future environmental-coupled capability portfolio. - Decision owner: human user/maintainer for product upgrades and configuration, present-task model for tactics. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider registry, SkillStore, extension hooks, update checker and project instructions. - Closure path: external package/version observation or user option → manual product/config choice; no built-in future intelligence appraisal decision returning into whole-current organizational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: proactive current task steps, ordinary skill loading or package update are not automatically S4. - Evidence: updater; extensions; skills; agent. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: explicit human/parent adaptation may exist in the external development org but is not a first-party session-governed S4 parent loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate identity/constitutional policy decision path for the agent organization is present in the shipped coding runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dangerous commands, write permissions, plugin trust and model identity instructions are tactical execution policy rather than ultimate organizational self-definition. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator chooses modes and confirmation, hook/config enforces declared constraints; no runtime S5 adjudicator decides continuing purpose/ultimate tradeoffs. - Decision owner: human for tool/trust config; deterministic policy checks for enforcement, without native S5 ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ApprovalPolicy, capability gates, HookRunner, provider identity guard and user-owned extension settings. - Closure path: configured mode/approval → permitted/blocked tool, not identity issue → legitimate parent/agent authority → binding whole-system policy return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: product identity prompt and permission modes don&#39;t confer ultimate organizational identity decision rights. - Evidence: policy; identity guard and tool loop; hooks; CLI config. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external humans maintain the software but their OSS governance is not operationally wired as session-level S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1a93f95e43fdf851192453f1c8e62b982b318d8a/assessments/deepseekfathom.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1a93f95e43fdf851192453f1c8e62b982b318d8a\">1a93f95 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-21T17:40:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maref-org/maref\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MAREF (Multi-Agent Recursive Evolution Framework)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-21\">2026-10-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maref-org/maref/tree/35b1d8108887bf162711c2212b7a67d6e21d9342\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 35b1d8108887bf162711c2212b7a67d6e21d9342\">35b1d81 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A configured model-backed first-party recursive code-change work cell produces substantive source changes and evaluates their acceptance for a developer-defined system evolution task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Nontrivial architectural refactoring, code quality regressions, test coverage and generated source correctness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In the normal configured OpenAI/Anthropic route, the model chooses task-semantic implementation text in response to a locally generated architecture proposal; the harness evaluates, deploys/rolls back and can observe another round. - Decision owner: LLMCodeGenerator with OpenAIProvider/AnthropicProvider supplies first-party invoked semantic code generation; the REL/SelfExecutor controls step admission and environment effects. External model compute does not own the harness architecture. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RecursiveEvolutionLoop OBSERVE→DIAGNOSE→ARCHITECT→CODEGEN→SAFETY→DEPLOY→VERIFY→EVALUATE; SelfObserver, SelfArchitect, LLMCodeGenerator, SelfExecutor, AST, tests, rollback and constitutional red-line guard. - Closure path: Local code and test observations become bounded architecture proposals; a model call generates change content; source is screened and deployed; verification feeds acceptance / rollback and further attempts. This is an operational loop with tool/file effects, not a static LLM API wrapper. - Boundary reachability: RecursiveEvolutionLoop.runsession() calls first-party observer/diagnostician/architect, then LLMCodeGenerator.generate, then SelfExecutor.deploy, then test/verification. The provider uses actual async OpenAI/Anthropic APIs when configured; absent keys, the fallback mock does not support a live cognitive claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Configured model-generated source changes are first-party invoked and assessed within a bounded work-cell execution path; model inference alone does not carry ownership. - Evidence: src/maref/recursive/recursiveevolutionloop.py; src/maref/recursive/selfobserver.py; src/maref/recursive/selfdiagnostician.py; src/maref/recursive/selfarchitect.py; src/maref/recursive/llmcodegenerator.py; src/maref/recursive/selfexecutor.py; src/maref/evolution/constitutionharness.py - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Attenuate reciprocal interference between multiple continuing, viable first-party agentic S1 units and return corrections to those units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent worker changes and conflicting ownership over shared work might require coordination. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A continuing coordinator would decide shared contention and return a changed action/commitment to distinct agent-owned S1 operations. - Decision owner: MultiAgentCoordinator dispatches caller-supplied BaseHarness objects and applies deterministic trust gates; no owner of reciprocal independent S1 operations is identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sequential/parallel task dispatch with trust check, optional circuit breaker, logging/metering and result aggregation; proposed role labels. - Closure path: runall fans one task out to registered harnesses and gathers results. It does not show collision detection across continuing S1 operations or a resolution that returns to those agents. Parallel implementation holds a lock around the whole dispatch, in fact serializing the supposed parallel work. - Boundary reachability: Do not credit S2 merely from multiagent naming, adapter integration or preflight safety gates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No material eligible first-party VSM function closure at the assessed organizational boundary; the cited mechanisms are narrower. - Evidence: src/maref/execution/multiagent/coordinator.py; src/maref/execution/harness/unified.py; src/maref/loop/bridge.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Regulate current whole-system resources, operational commitments and exceptions across multiple viable operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Cost/safety interruptions, agent failures, task timeouts and backlog would need system-level managerial decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose/revise global priorities or resource commitments, assign authority and return the decision to ongoing S1 units. - Decision owner: Deterministic FSM and circuit breakers enforce pre-supplied bounds on individual runtime/task loops; no S3 steward/controller with whole-system decision feedback established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Gray-code governance FSM, GovernedLoop, per-execution circuit breaker, identity, tool permission, safety/quality gates and per-task cost caps. - Closure path: Stages advance along predefined local paths, or stop when preflight, cost, safety or test conditions fail. This is current-task execution safety, not an evidenced whole-of-organization management relation across ongoing S1 units. - Boundary reachability: Governance code is real and executable, but an implementation of constraints is not itself an S3 decision owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No material eligible first-party VSM function closure at the assessed organizational boundary; the cited mechanisms are narrower. - Evidence: src/maref/governance/statemachine.py; src/maref/loop/bridge.py; src/maref/execution/harness/unified.py; src/maref/execution/multiagent/coordinator.py; src/maref/codegen/loop.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Complement routine managerial reports with an independent audit of operational reality and deliver discrepancies to an S3 management owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A first-party agent&#39;s own completion/health claims might be inaccurate or misleading. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently inspect across reporting lines, judge discrepancies, and return findings to current organizational management for corrective intervention. - Decision owner: First-party AST/test/quality checkers, HMAC audit logs and verification registries primarily attest task/work-product facts; no independent complementary S3 reviewer and S3 feedback owner are evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Audit hash chains, snapshot/provenance and verifier consensus, source lint/tests, separate ratchet configuration guard. - Closure path: A failed code check blocks/rolls back the same code-change pipeline; its result does not challenge an S3 manager&#39;s global report through a distinct organizational channel. - Boundary reachability: No material S3 relation established; source verifies code/safety and records transitions, but this does not prove audit of an S3 account. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No material eligible first-party VSM function closure at the assessed organizational boundary; the cited mechanisms are narrower. - Evidence: src/maref/recursive/recursiveevolutionloop.py; src/maref/recursive/selfexecutor.py; src/maref/execution/harness/unified.py; src/maref/evolution/constitutionharness.py; src/maref/governance/statemachine.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Construct prospective changes to the harness&#39;s own future coding/verification capability using observed system health, architecture and proposed source evolution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing module coupling, code coverage, recurring test failures and new technology constraints on future runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select an adaptation candidate targeting future capabilities and route it through bounded safety/deploy/verify; no separate independent S4 strategy authority is demonstrated. - Decision owner: The SelfArchitect and REL pipeline form first-party prospective options; the model may generate code for a candidate, but constitution/safety and transaction guards bound effect and do not constitute an independent strategic S4 regulator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SelfObserver.observecodebase/test coverage, SelfDiagnostician, SelfArchitect.proposeall, code generation, transactional deploy, tests, divergence detector and rollback. - Closure path: Observations about the system&#39;s own code/test environment generate future codebase improvement options and can lead to deployed capability modifications after checks; a subsequent observer could evaluate the changed codebase. Unlike mere per-task repair, this path targets the framework&#39;s future reusable code and tests. - Boundary reachability: RecursiveEvolutionLoop.runsession wires OBSERVE→DIAGNOSE→ARCHITECT→CODEGEN→DEPLOY→VERIFY→EVALUATE on the actual source tree. This supports a constructor-level prospective adaptation capability, not established stable autonomous S4 organizational governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The default runtime implements a concrete reusable prospective adaptation constructor but not independently owned strategic S4 authority. - Evidence: src/maref/recursive/recursiveevolutionloop.py; src/maref/recursive/selfobserver.py; src/maref/recursive/selfdiagnostician.py; src/maref/recursive/selfarchitect.py; src/maref/recursive/llmcodegenerator.py; src/maref/recursive/selfexecutor.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: moderate - Caveats: A test/coverage driven self-modifier is narrower than environmental market/strategy forecasting. It does not prove independent future-option appraisal or safe live deployment; S4=A would be unsupported.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimately arbitrate ultimate identity/purpose and constitutional policy for the agent organization and return it as operative authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicts about permitted autonomous modification, safety red lines, final system identity and legitimate governance changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A legitimate S5 decision owner must adopt/revise ultimate policy and make an operative authority change that governs future S1 behavior. - Decision owner: A ConstitutionHarness compares strings and actor labels against fixed protected paths; there is no authenticated first-party organizational constitutional decision owner or policy-change transaction in the assessed runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ConstitutionHarness, freeze zones, safety state machine, rule/policy defaults and immutable-file restrictions. - Closure path: checkchange returns allowed/blocked for source modifications based on hard-coded rule lists, with actor=&#39;humanconstitutionmaker&#39; exempting selected red-line tests; neither verified human authority nor ultimate system-level policy adoption and return is shown. - Boundary reachability: Literal human-role string, governance slogans and static red-lines cannot be reclassified as a legitimate parent S5 function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No material eligible first-party VSM function closure at the assessed organizational boundary; the cited mechanisms are narrower. - Evidence: src/maref/evolution/constitutionharness.py; src/maref/governance/statemachine.py; README.md; src/maref/recursive/recursiveevolutionloop.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/926c22a06b83689fdd1f331894a7e2aa6dff84f7/assessments/ma/maref.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 926c22a06b83689fdd1f331894a7e2aa6dff84f7\">926c22a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T01:01:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Onipounds/agent-loop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-loop</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Onipounds/agent-loop/tree/49525dc12a9861f71c99050de3b4a4b8b897e9fa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 49525dc12a9861f71c99050de3b4a4b8b897e9fa\">49525dc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform model-guided goal-facing telemetry/calculation queries through a first-party multi-turn tool/result/answer feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended user wording within model capability, different telemetry results, requested intermediate computations, tool errors, numerical hallucination and finite step budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured external model, reached through first-party Model.respond and runtime.run, chooses next tool names/arguments or final text after seeing tool outcomes and verification rejection feedback. - Decision owner: The model behind AnthropicModel owns the substantive per-task next-action discretion in the supported real-mode composition; user owns the top-level task and allowed tool environment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry, JSON input schemas, exception capture, in-memory transcript, numeric grounding/recomputation, maxsteps and CLI error status. - Closure path: User goal → model choice → first-party tool execution → tool results in Memory → model&#39;s next decision; when final text appears, verify feedback either accepts the outcome or returns a correction signal to the same task loop. - Boundary reachability: CLI real mode instantiates AnthropicModel and calls the actual runtime.run. The Model protocol and tool-result writes are concretely connected in the shipped code, rather than merely documented as a future integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the real model leaves a bounded deterministic offline script and tool/verification infrastructure but no general adaptive model-guided task choice. The first-party harness implements the iterative operating feedback path, while the configured external model supplies the operational choice. - Evidence: agentloop/runtime.py; agentloop/models.py; agentloop/cli.py; agentloop/tools.py; agentloop/verify.py; tests/testagent.py. - Basis: Explicit published model/tool integration plus structural wiring; external model success and reliability not empirically tested. - Confidence: High for this shipped control path; lower for performance of a live model/provider that was not executed. - Caveats: The verifier&#39;s &#39;verified&#39; flag is confined to its numeric heuristics and must not be generalized to total task validity; offline FakeModel is not independent proof of model autonomy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately owned coordination function balancing mutual disturbances among two or more distinct viable S1 operating departments was identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A run may contain several requested tools, but tool timing, input errors, task-step limits and result ordering remain local to the one S1 task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party S2-specific decision adjusting the relations/commitments of distinct S1 units; serial tool execution and shared transcript are mechanics. - Decision owner: One task model directs tool selection, and ToolRegistry executes; no distinct coordination owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Registry, step iteration, tool result list and task transcript. - Closure path: Individual tool outcomes return into the same S1 model context; no inter-S1 disturbance sensing → negotiation/attenuation → feedback into two separate operating units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An individual tool is not a viable S1 division and no S2-specific owner appears just because one model sequences calls. - Evidence: agentloop/runtime.py; agentloop/tools.py; agentloop/memory.py; README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative: first-party standard-distribution execution is one run with cooperating tools. - Confidence: High at this boundary. - Caveats: External orchestration could compose multiple independent instances and add S2, but this is not shipped by this repository.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-current executive with a portfolio view and discretionary resource, obligation or priority control across operating S1 divisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Step exhaustion, incorrect task replies and failing tool calls change individual-run execution; they do not establish whole-organization allocation decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The CLI caller chooses maxsteps and the verifier deterministically rejects locally invalid answers; neither makes discretionary cross-S1 current-management decisions. - Decision owner: Host/user configures the task and step budget; task model handles its own loop; verifier enforces a limited predicate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxsteps termination, CLI exit code, checker callback, tool error handling. - Closure path: A candidate task answer → local verifier → retry or termination of that task. No distinct managerial current-state view, managerial decision, and returned commitments to independent S1 divisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The real run uses internal operational feedback, not a separate authorized executive that would survive removal of the model task owner. There is no operational parent-governed S3 return into a whole-system current-control decision. - Evidence: agentloop/runtime.py; agentloop/verify.py; agentloop/cli.py; agentloop/evals.py. - Basis: Bounded negative after distinguishing run guards/QA from current organizational control. - Confidence: High for inspected distribution. - Caveats: Human choice of CLI flags, task and provider is external ordinary operator configuration, not evidence of S3=P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently mandated complementary reality audit of ordinary reporting to a distinct S3 manager with returned corrective-control authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A numerical answer can misstate telemetry aggregates, and a test can observe an incorrect output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: verify() checks the same in-loop answer against data already in that run&#39;s working transcript. It can reject and retry within S1, but does not independently audit S3 reporting via a complementary source. - Decision owner: The deterministic S1 verifier, invoked synchronously from runtime.run, has no separate auditor mandate/organizational owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Numeric grounding, raw-list recomputation, tests, offline eval and in-memory tool-results log. - Closure path: Tool outputs → same-run verification → model rejection/retry. Not a complementary operational reality path into an independent S3 executive and subsequent management correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even though verification is separate Python code, it acts as the task&#39;s immediate acceptance predicate. Removing a hypothetical complementary auditor changes nothing: no separate S3 or audit-return mandate is wired in. - Evidence: agentloop/verify.py; agentloop/runtime.py; agentloop/evals.py; tests/testagent.py. - Basis: Source-specific bounded negative; does not conflate QA with S3. - Confidence: High at this assessed recursion. - Caveats: Third-party auditors could inspect tool logs outside the package; such a system is not included.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party function observes changing external environments to generate or commit prospective modifications to future operating capability or strategy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar future tasks or changing sensor conditions could motivate changed tools, models or evaluation coverage. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The task model reasons about the current question, while developer-provided tools, model selection and offline scripts remain fixed. Evals score current sessions; no identified option-selection and future capability update closes back into operation. - Decision owner: User/developer decides future software changes externally; the deployed loop uses the currently supplied tools and model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Evaluations, optional model selection, scripted offline planner, README scaling roadmap and parameterized tools. - Closure path: Task → current answer/evaluation output; no outside intelligence → prospective adaptation option → authorized decision → changed subsequent tool/policy/runtime capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An external model&#39;s choice of the next task action is S1 operating feedback, not a distinct organizational future-adaptation right. An eval score is not evidence that the package rewrites its own capability. No implemented parent-governed S4 feedback return is shown. - Evidence: agentloop/evals.py; agentloop/runtime.py; agentloop/models.py; agentloop/offline.py; README.md. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting runtime, evals, supported modes and future-roadmap distinction. - Confidence: Medium-high; code is small and modes are explicit. - Caveats: An integrator might manually use evaluations to develop a new version, outside the assessed first-party supported operating loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party constitutional authority that decides the system&#39;s identity, ultimate purpose or governing policy and returns a binding policy choice into subsequent operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task goals, allowed tools, provider credentials and model settings require authority boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The CLI user/developer supplies the goal/provider/configuration; ToolRegistry grants a fixed set of tools and runtime checks ordinary execution budgets. No higher-recursion policy matter reaches a first-party legitimate parent S5 decision-and-return loop. - Decision owner: External user/developer owns purpose and package design; runtime delegates task action choice to the model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI parameters, pyproject packaging, fixed tool schemas, MIT license, README governance and numeric task acceptance. - Closure path: Operator task and existing configured policies → one run; there is no explicit identity/policy issue → legitimate parent authoritative deliberation/decision → binding return changing future first-party system operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool limits and a human-selected task do not give the agent or an implemented parent mode the ultimate identity/policy right. Repository maintainers own changes to source externally but are not a packaged parent decision loop for the assessed agent&#39;s organizational identity. - Evidence: agentloop/cli.py; agentloop/tools.py; agentloop/runtime.py; README.md; pyproject.toml. - Basis: Bounded negative for a single-run task harness and its supported CLI distribution. - Confidence: High for this scoped claim. - Caveats: Human project ownership/license is real but is not evidence of S5=P under this operational boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/212837b40b31341b8e4493f347ac13e165bce60b/assessments/ag/agent-loop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 212837b40b31341b8e4493f347ac13e165bce60b\">212837b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T18:04:41+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ominiverdi/ferrum\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ferrum</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ominiverdi/ferrum/tree/f31151d34fade3ef88104a33ba2a4042e689be35\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f31151d34fade3ef88104a33ba2a4042e689be35\">f31151d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software-engineering work on the selected workspace by interpreting the task, inspecting project evidence, selecting coding/tool actions, applying or running them and reacting to returned results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous source trees and project instructions, incomplete task information, file contents, search/command output, tool failures, changing workspace state and implementation choices encountered while completing coding work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what project evidence to inspect, which exposed coding action/tool to invoke next, what modifications or commands to attempt, and when enough work/evidence exists to produce the final response within externally selected policy limits. - Decision owner: the model-backed coding actor instantiated by Ferrum&#39;s standard agent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime/system/project context construction; native/MCP tool registry; provider adapters; safety/tool/root policy; durable session/history; compaction; loop guard; cancellation; interactive/print/ACP transport. - Closure path: user task/workspace context → provider request → model selects tool/action → Ferrum executes or rejects the action → tool/environment result is appended to the session context → the same model-backed actor receives the result and selects the next action or terminates. - Boundary reachability: ferrum, ferrum -p and each supported ACP session directly instantiate the first-party agent/tool loop; no application-authored orchestration layer is needed to reach the coding actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed actor while retaining tools, policies and session machinery removes the open-ended coding judgment that selects and sequences repository-facing work. - Evidence: docs/spec.md; docs/tools.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider/model inference remains an external dependency; Ferrum is credited for the shipped role/tool/feedback composition, not provider internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination function was established at the selected recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S2 level because one active Ferrum agent session exposes one primary model-backed S1 rather than multiple distinct S1 operational units with a concrete mutual interference/oscillation relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: safe read-only tool batches may execute concurrently; mutating/mixed/MCP batches stay sequential; ACP can host multiple independent sessions; session persistence uses file locking. These are execution/session mechanisms rather than a demonstrated coordination relation among S1 units inside one organization. - Closure path: not applicable; no distinct-S1 disturbance → attenuation → changed subsequent S1 behavior loop exists in the reviewed standard session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel read/ls/grep/find calls are tools chosen by the same coding actor. Concurrent ACP sessions are documented as independent agent sessions, so their co-residence in one server process does not create subordinate S1s at the selected session recursion. - Evidence: docs/tools.md; docs/acp.md; docs/spec.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this classification is recursion-sensitive; a separately defined external organization composed from several Ferrum ACP sessions could require a different assessment, but Ferrum does not supply that organization at the reviewed boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-organization current-control function distinct from the coding S1 and deterministic loop/runtime safeguards was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S3 ownership level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. The adaptive loop guard can detect repeated calls/errors and alter the next request, while cancellation, tool-round limits and policy can stop/limit work; these mechanisms regulate one S1&#39;s execution according to encoded/operator policy rather than manage current commitments/resources across an organization of S1 units. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: adaptive loop guard; optional maxtoolrounds; cancellation; safety tiers; tool/root policy; session/ACP active-turn limits; foreground wait bounds. - Closure path: not applicable; no whole-organization current view plus substantive resource/priority/commitment/accountability intervention path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the loop guard&#39;s response is deterministic runtime policy and survives conceptually without an autonomous management actor; model action after guard guidance remains the same S1&#39;s operational continuation rather than S3 management. - Evidence: docs/tools.md; docs/spec.md; docs/acp.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strong lifecycle/safety enforcement is not denied; it does not meet the Profile&#39;s stronger S3 organizational-control threshold at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary audit role with sufficiently independent access, audit judgment and corrective return into the supported runtime was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S3 level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. Ferrum can run tests/commands as normal S1 tools and its repository includes benchmark validators, but the standard coding distribution does not wire a separate reviewer/judge actor to challenge S1 conclusions and return an audit verdict into current control. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordinary bash/tool verification, deterministic safety validation and repository bench/ task validators are operation/development evidence rather than an independent runtime audit owner. - Closure path: not applicable; no supported independent challenge → audit judgment → corrective-control return path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a coding model may choose to run tests or inspect its own work, but that remains S1 self-checking. Repository benchmark scripts are adjacent project-development evaluation and are not boundary-reachable decision owners in ordinary Ferrum sessions. - Evidence: README.md; docs/tools.md; bench/. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic validation and self-verification can materially improve quality without satisfying S3 independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party prospective environment-intelligence and organizational adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S4 level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. Ferrum can load current project instructions/skills, consult session history, use MCP/tools and summarize old context, but these mechanisms support the present coding operation rather than model future environmental change and persistently alter Ferrum&#39;s organizational capability/strategy. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS/context files; skills; MCP; history search/read; durable sessions; context compaction; user/provider/model configuration; foreground wait monitoring. - Closure path: not applicable; no prospective scan → adaptation-option formation → persistent capability/strategy change → later operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: compaction changes the representation of current conversation history, and history/skills expose information to S1. The separate background-task document explicitly states that durable/model-owned background tasks are deferred exploration rather than implemented behavior. - Evidence: docs/spec.md; docs/tools.md; docs/background-tasks.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: present-task learning/replanning and persistent conversational state are not sufficient S4 evidence under Profile 0.2.4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. User/global/project configuration chooses provider/model, system prompt, safety, tool/root/MCP/skill limits and the task itself; project policy may narrow authority but cannot autonomously redefine Ferrum&#39;s identity/purpose. - Decision owner: not established inside the assessed organization; ultimate purpose/policy remains developer/operator/project-config owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: safety tiers; [tools] allow/deny and writable roots; project restrictive policy; system prompt override; ACP optional permission UX; credential/protected-target guards; cancellation and tool limits. - Closure path: not applicable; no identity/ultimate-policy proposal/dispute reaches an autonomous or qualifying parent S5 decision path and returns as an authoritative policy change to later operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime strongly enforces selected limits, but enforcement is distinct from ownership. ACP client permission can only restrict an operation Ferrum already authorizes and is ordinary operational approval, not an identity-level parent governance loop. - Evidence: README.md; docs/spec.md; docs/acp.md; docs/tools.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: restrictive project policy and client permissions are important governance/safety boundaries, but generic configuration/approval does not satisfy S5 identity closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d1313c4f47597c6c7244423827f1de5b7f68f669/assessments/ferrum.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d1313c4f47597c6c7244423827f1de5b7f68f669\">d1313c4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T22:33:41+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Fenix46/LuckyCLI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LuckyCLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Fenix46/LuckyCLI/tree/496c89124c9a349c36337df35df6581533e26581\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 496c89124c9a349c36337df35df6581533e26581\">496c891 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute a coding request through iterative native model-directed file/command/search/graph actions and optional independently operated subagent subtasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes in requested repository work, incomplete context, tool errors, failed verification, codebase structure, model errors and return reports from delegated subtasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next first-party tool/delegated task, respond to its result, alter the work path, and decide when the current request is sufficiently complete. - Decision owner: model running within first-party Agent.send of the root or a separately instantiated sequential subordinate Agent. The local provider supplies inference but does not own the tool orchestration or terminal workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, model transport, transcript/context compaction, permission callback, subagent profile registry, subagent bridge, graph enrichment and ordinary test checks. - Closure path: user request → model stream with tool calls → local first-party dispatcher → filesystem/shell/graph or child-agent report → concrete tool result appended to active conversation → model chooses another action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: packages/cli/src/runtime.ts builds the main Agent for installed CLI and ACP/TUI; packages/core/src/agent/agent.ts directly executes the tool/result continuation; Root.tsx creates a reachable child delegation bridge. No third-party coding-agent process is required for the owned S1 loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model&#39;s chosen tool call/delegation and interpretation of returned observations, runtime still enforces safety and verifies scripts but cannot autonomously decide the next coding operation. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agent/agent.ts; packages/cli/src/runtime.ts; packages/cli/src/ui/Root.tsx; packages/core/src/agents/runner.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/spawn-agent.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/index.ts. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high from first-party loop implementations. - Caveats: evidence establishes a native operational feedback right but not live benchmark performance or universal working provider credentials.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no native inter-S1 interference/oscillation regulation relation among simultaneously active first-party operational units was established at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: subagent write conflicts are avoided by not running peers at the same time; there is no separate active multi-worker collision that a dedicated coordinating mechanism observes and attenuates with feedback to both agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: main agent chooses the next sequential delegate; the runner completes one child before returning. No peer-claim arbitration, shared multiple-writer lock, cross-agent collision feedback or active conflict attenuation decision owner is supported. - Decision owner: root operational task agent decides delegation, while deterministic child invocation serializes; neither establishes S2-specific feedback across concurrently active S1s. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static serial subagent runner, child tools exclusion list, task list, tool permission limits and request/response child summaries. - Closure path: root awaits completion of one child → report returned → next task selected by the root as an S1 action; no distinct inter-unit disturbances or compensating S2 loop are presented to active peers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-guided sequencing of one current child is part of the operational task and does not become distinct S2 coordination merely because the tool is called spawnagent. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agents/runner.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/spawn-agent.ts; packages/core/src/tasks/store.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/tasks.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: if external users run several installations concurrently, their external coordination is not a native first-party LuckyCLI function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independently owned whole-current organization oversight of simultaneously operating S1 units, commitments and resources was established beyond one user&#39;s coding objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the root may split a project into serial subtasks, track local work progress and choose different providers, but that is one S1&#39;s delegated implementation rather than system-wide management of multiple ongoing operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select a named child profile/task, update the local task list and request user approvals; none is a demonstrated whole-current authority over an independent operational workforce. - Decision owner: the root coder for current task progress, user for consent. No separate first-party S3 management decision right at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: local tasks, plans, sequential delegations, checkpoint/snapshot and review tool outputs, usage/status panel. - Closure path: child completion or tool error → root task agent continues ordinary work; no distinct current-organization decision over multiple active S1 resource/commitment obligations is returned to those units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: although a model chooses local work order, it does not exercise whole-current control over an operational organization separate from the one coding task. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agents/runner.ts; packages/core/src/agent/plan.ts; packages/core/src/tasks/store.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/tasks.ts; packages/cli/src/ui/Root.tsx; packages/cli/src/ui/components/TaskPanel.tsx. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: generic task planning and model/provider configuration do not establish S3; an external project manager may coordinate multiple independent LuckyCLI instances outside this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit judgment/alternative access path that returns findings to a distinct current-control owner is established in this first-party coding installation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: generated code may fail tests or differ from changed Git diffs; those risks are addressed by ordinary first-party coding verification or user-initiated review and do not establish a separate complementary organizational inquiry. - Decisive decision or feedback right: current coding loop can choose a verify tool or run configured after-edit tests; the user can invoke explicit review/verify commands, but no independent audit actor takes an alternative evidentiary mandate and returns an S3 finding to a metasystemic current decision owner. - Decision owner: project scripts and in-band model supply tests/review text; the producing root agent and/or user decide subsequent ordinary task steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trusted project-script execution, structured result/exit code, mandatory re-entry to the tool transcript after editing when enabled, diff collection, JSON review schema, manual /review and lucky review. - Closure path: edit → ordinary script/check status appended to that very tool result (or user-initiated diff review in the same model&#39;s session) → coder may retry the failing edit. This is useful testing but not a sufficiently independent, complementary audit-control path beyond routine production checks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct raw tests can contradict a current edit&#39;s apparent success, but after-edit is an in-band routine QA hook with no independent auditor selecting substantive evidence or returning a distinct whole-current audit finding. The manual lucky review command presents findings to its caller; it does not wire an S3→S3 control return into ongoing agent execution. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agent/agent.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/verify.ts; packages/core/src/workflow/verify.ts; packages/core/src/workflow/review.ts; packages/cli/src/ui/commands/workflow.ts; packages/cli/src/workflow-cli.ts; packages/core/src/tools/builtin/index.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is not a claim that separate human review or tests are unnecessary or ineffective, only that their witnessed ownership/return does not establish the Profile&#39;s organizational S3 at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective outside-and-then process that creates future capability adaptation alternatives and returns them to current control is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future provider/model availability, changing source structure and codebase knowledge may matter to the developer, but LuckyCLI code reviewed uses them to improve current task context and route immediate inference/tool operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: models choose current coding work and provider configurations; deterministic graph index and skill matching gather relevant current repository facts. No owned long-horizon environmental intelligence decision or adaptation-option/return is found. - Decision owner: current task coder or user for model updates and settings, not a separate first-party S4 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project knowledge graph build/query/update, skill graph matching, context enrichment, update policy/staging, provider catalog and session history. - Closure path: updated graph facts or installed user-selected release influence current/future prompt context, but no external prospective investigation → adaptation choice → S3 system capability adjustment loop is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistent memory, code graph refresh and user-initiated software updates are helpful facilities but not prospective organizational intelligence in the VSM sense. - Evidence: packages/core/src/graph/enrich.ts; packages/core/src/graph/update.ts; packages/core/src/skills/graph.ts; packages/core/src/update/policy.ts; packages/cli/src/update.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: project users may use the tool to research future options, but such tasks alone are not native ongoing system-level adaptation authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party highest-authority identity/ethos decision and binding governance return exists at the installed agent organization boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local tool risk, provider permissions, user trust and session approval, updates and model selection are handled by configured execution constraints instead of an ultimate-identity dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human user grants/denies a tool action, software enforces tool permission and approved command scopes; no discretionary S3–S4 identity-level resolution with durable returned policy. - Decision owner: user for ordinary consent and provider/config settings, deterministic runtime for enforcement, not a verified first-party S5 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool permission policy, approveTool callback, remembered approvals, session trust prompt, plan presentation and update rules. - Closure path: action request is allowed/denied and current coding operation continues; no exceptional mission/identity issue reaches a legitimate highest-level authority and returns a binding system-level rule. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary approval and persistent configuration are not policy-identity arbitration; no parent governance is inferred solely from user ownership of settings. - Evidence: packages/core/src/agent/agent.ts; packages/cli/src/approval.ts; packages/cli/src/ui/TrustPrompt.tsx; packages/core/src/agent/plan.ts; packages/core/src/update/policy.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human/enterprise organization may govern installations externally; its policy may constrain the program but that does not create a first-party S5 return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5bf3826fa02de7383eec5063ace990630eb82dc3/assessments/luckycli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5bf3826fa02de7383eec5063ace990630eb82dc3\">5bf3826 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T03:05:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/reflex-agent/reflex-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reflex</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/reflex-agent/reflex-agent/tree/f3b3dd7b438c8333e5731a29023eca81239c7d19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f3b3dd7b438c8333e5731a29023eca81239c7d19\">f3b3dd7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-driven execution of user/project work through persistent Reflex topics, delegated specialist turns, tasks and scheduled/headless runs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended user requests, project/file state, tool results, specialist findings, task progress, permissions and workflow-triggered work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose how to pursue the requested outcome within the Reflex topic, including whether/how to delegate specialist work, what returned evidence to synthesize and which first-party action markers to emit. - Decision owner: autonomous model-driven operating actor running under Reflex&#39;s first-party orchestrator protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent topics/events, specialist dispatch protocol, task records, bounded /goal continuation, worktree cwd binding, host tools, permissions and headless execution. - Closure path: model decision → Reflex action/dispatch/tool execution → changed project/task/environment state or child-agent result → observation/reinvocation → subsequent model decision/output. - Boundary reachability: default interactive topics directly reach the Reflex orchestrator turn; task dispatch and scheduled/headless workflows also reach the same first-party operating path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: once an operating turn starts, the model actor has bounded discretion over decomposition/delegation/action and consumes returned results; Reflex supplies and closes that organizational protocol. Claude Code/Codex may execute the model/tool substrate, but no internal metasystem semantics from those external subprocesses are imported. - Evidence: lib/reflex/prompts/defaults.ts; lib/server/agents/headless.ts; lib/server/tasks/dispatch.ts; topic/goal manager path. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the positive claim is Reflex&#39;s first-party operating organization around the model actor, not a claim that Claude Code/Codex internals belong to Reflex.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference between concurrent code-task S1 units that would otherwise mutate the same repository checkout/files. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent agent tasks operating on one repository can collide through shared file mutations, branch/checkout state and commits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a disturbance-specific coordination arrangement that separates concurrent code-task mutation lanes before task execution begins. - Decision owner: Reflex supplies and deterministically enforces the worktree isolation constructor path; no autonomous first-party coordinator owns discretionary S2 policy across competing units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: unique task/&lt;slug&gt; branch/worktree allocation, destination/branch collision checks, worktree-bound task cwd and explicit later merge path. - Closure path: code task selected → Reflex creates isolated worktree/branch → dispatched task agent is instructed and launched inside that checkout → subsequent file operations/commits occur outside main/shared checkout → later controlled merge reconciles output. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordination function and closure are first-party, but the decisive isolation policy is deterministic host enforcement rather than an autonomous coordinator that observes competing units and chooses/adjusts coordination policy. This supports C, not A. - Evidence: lib/server/tasks/worktree.ts; lib/server/tasks/dispatch.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic multi-agent dispatch, dependency fields and shared task state are not separately counted as S2. - Distinct S1 units: separate task-bound model/agent executions can concurrently perform code work against the same project repository. - Inter-S1 disturbance: tasks/worktree.ts explicitly states that each code task runs in its own checkout so tasks do not collide on files; a shared checkout would expose one task to another task&#39;s mutations/branch state. - Attenuating coordination relation: Reflex allocates a unique worktree and branch per code task before starting the task agent, thereby separating mutation lanes. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: dispatchTask binds the agent cwd to the isolated worktree and prompts it to commit there, so later S1 file operations are changed by the coordination decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the evidence names a concrete cross-S1 file-collision disturbance and a first-party isolation response whose purpose is to prevent that disturbance, rather than merely routing or delegating work. - Boundary reachability: standard task dispatch automatically reaches the worktree-isolation path for code tasks in Git repositories.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: whole-Space current supervision and intervention over ongoing commitments, running agent work and blocked permission decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous current commitments, runaway/stale work, blocked agent turns, requested privileges and operational intervention needs across a project Space. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether current goals/work continue, are stopped/abandoned, and whether requested permissions are allowed or denied. - Decision owner: legitimate parent user/owner operating the first-party Reflex control surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: root dashboard aggregation, active-goal/running-agent views, pending-approval aggregation, agent-manager stop path, goal clearing, permission response bridge and explicit task/worktree merge controls. - Closure path: Reflex surfaces whole-Space current work or a blocked decision → user selects stop/abandon/allow/deny or enters the relevant work item for intervention → Reflex manager/store applies the decision → waiting/running S1 operation changes or terminates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reflex supplies the whole-Space view and enforces returned interventions, but no autonomous supervisor is established with equivalent authority to reprioritize/stop/authorize work across the Space. The parent user owns the decisive S3 right. - Evidence: lib/server/dashboard-actions.ts; app/roots/[id]/components/dashboard-active-goals.tsx; app/roots/[id]/components/dashboard-pending-approvals.tsx; lib/server/topic-actions.ts; lib/server/agents/manager.ts; app/api/agents/[agentId]/respond/route.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: local topic decisions and ordinary orchestrator delegation remain S1 activity; they are not promoted to S3 by the manager or dispatcher names. - Whole-system current view: the root dashboard aggregates active persistent goals, currently running agents without goals and pending permission/question/MCP interactions across every topic of the Space, giving the parent a cross-operating-unit current view. - Current-control decision scope: stop/delete running topics, abandon persistent goals, answer blocked questions, allow/deny requested operations and retain explicit control over merging task work into the main repository state. - Boundary reachability: these are ordinary first-party dashboard/chat/runtime surfaces in the standard distribution, and permission decisions are returned through the live manager to waiting agent execution.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no qualifying complementary independent audit function is established at the declared Reflex Space boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the review looked for operational claims or conditions that are independently checked through a distinct evidence path rather than merely logged by the same runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party independent auditor was found that judges discrepancies and returns them into S3 current control. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: utility audit JSONL, agent event logs, task observation/status and dashboard aggregation provide observability but not the required independent audit relation. - Closure path: host/API calls can be logged and later viewed, but no distinct ordinary-path claim → complementary reality check → audit judgment → corrective current-control feedback loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the utility audit is emitted by the same host execution path it records; neither it nor event/status presentation establishes an independently judging audit actor. - Evidence: lib/server/utilities/audit.ts; app/audit/page.tsx; lib/server/tasks/observe.ts; dashboard/event surfaces. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: auditability/logging is useful evidence infrastructure but is not equivalent to S3 organizational ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no complete first-party outside-and-then environmental intelligence/adaptation loop is established at the pinned revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the review looked for external/future-relevant change that is deliberately sensed, converted into adaptation options, decided upon and returned into current capability/operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor is established with a closed right to choose prospective capability adaptation from external/future distinctions and install that choice into subsequent operating repertoire. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow scheduler, headless agent execution, generated utilities, searchable memory, weekly memory rollup and on-demand dashboard AI suggestions can support operation/adaptation but do not establish the required S4 closure themselves. - Closure path: configured workflows execute and memory/suggestions summarize internal/past/current state, but no evidenced external/future sensing → adaptation option → decision → present capability change → later S1/S3 use loop is closed by the standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model suggestions and utility generation may be model-driven, but at the pinned revision their evidenced triggers concern current user/project needs and require composition/user action; they do not establish an agent-owned prospective environmental-intelligence loop. - Evidence: lib/server/workflows/scheduler.ts; lib/server/workflows/system-tasks.ts; lib/server/ai-suggestions.ts; utility generation/update/host surfaces inspected in the pinned tree. - Basis: structural absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: downstream user-authored workflows could create an S4 loop, but each such specialization is a separate evidence/boundary question and is not inherited by the standalone repository.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no qualifying ultimate-policy or system-identity closure is established for the Reflex Space organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the review looked for questions about the Space&#39;s ultimate purpose/identity/policy that are distinguished from ordinary operating configuration and permission choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party path identifies such an identity/policy issue, routes it to an actor with explicit ultimate authority and closes the returned decision into subsequent organizational identity. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompt templates, model assignments, permission settings, workflow definitions and project settings constrain operation but remain configuration/support surfaces. - Closure path: users can edit prompts/settings and approve tools, but no evidenced identity issue → ultimate-policy judgment → durable identity update → later organizational behavior loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no autonomous or parent-governed S5 actor/path is established; ordinary owner configuration is not upgraded to S5 merely because it affects behavior. - Evidence: lib/reflex/prompts/defaults.ts; settings/model assignment surfaces; permission response path; workflow/task controls; project dashboard surfaces. - Basis: structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream Space may embed identity policy in user-authored prompts/workflows, but that downstream specialization is outside this standalone repository assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3d75bbc6fa7ba5292840a4d022d6622838c7acdc/assessments/reflex.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3d75bbc6fa7ba5292840a4d022d6622838c7acdc\">3d75bbc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-10T20:55:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rohaquinlop/michin\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MichiN</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-10\">2026-10-10</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rohaquinlop/michin/tree/ff001031aad8486583055455989e02d2a88c8518\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ff001031aad8486583055455989e02d2a88c8518\">ff00103 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute first-party model-selected source and shell tools for project coding work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user requirements, source state, compile errors, returned tool failures and environmental constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select contingent source-edit/read/search/bash actions and decide subsequent work after observing outcomes. - Decision owner: The coding LLM operating through MichiN&#39;s own Agent/loop, with action rights bounded by native tool/policy enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider streaming, native tool dispatcher, guardrails, context history, session state and user approval/mode choices. - Closure path: User coding request → inference chooses tool action → first-party native tool executes on workspace → returned result enters core state → model chooses next corrective action or finishes. - Boundary reachability: The shipped CLI, TUI, prompt and RPC operation call the first-party Rust agent core and first-party concrete tools. External providers supply inference only, not an external coding harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides how to absorb local coding variety; native tools and policies enforce its chosen bounded work. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/agent.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/loopmod.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/tools.rs; crates/michin/src/tools/write.rs; crates/michin/src/tools/bash.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: This is evidence of operative autonomy, not a measured benchmark-quality claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party organizational coordination loop across two independently functioning operational S1 cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent tool execution may affect one active coding session, but does not evidence competing autonomous work units whose variety must be mutually stabilized. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None across distinct S1 units; generic tool execution mode is selected/obeyed inside the single coder. - Decision owner: No separate S2 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Parallel or sequential native tool execution, queued user messages, session-level cancellation. - Closure path: Tool output returns to the one model/agent; no inter-S1 interference detection and feedback to another S1 is seen. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parallel file reads, serialized edit calls, model switching and queueing do not constitute an inter-agent coordination relationship. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/tools.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/agent.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/loopmod.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Separately running MichiN processes would need a separately established first-party organizational connection.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately owned whole-current regulator that supervises and reallocates work across multiple operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Run interruption, latency, current budget, repeated model/tool requests and execution errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User steers or cancels; native runtime enforces timeout/repetition bounds and local safety policy. - Decision owner: Deterministic single-session runtime and human task operator, not an S3 actor with discretionary whole-current view. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Active-run mutex, cancellation tokens, run reports, tool watchdog, turn termination statuses and model escalation. - Closure path: Stop/steer/escalate changes this agent&#39;s present tool turn, without manager-mediated system-wide current allocation across multiple S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Status reports, execution ceilings and individual agent control do not transfer broader managerial judgment to the model. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/agent.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/events.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/loopmod.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/commandpolicy.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: External human management of a coding team lies outside this packaged local session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No material independent operational-audit owner with corrective return to coding S1. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe commands and possibly defective code require checks, but safety enforcement does not itself establish complementary audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic command safety rejects destructive actions; optional Rhai scripts can pre-block or post-observe tools, not act as a separately autonomous supplementary auditor by default. - Decision owner: Static runtime policy and user-configured scripts; original coder retains responsibility for correction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Before/after tool hooks, Rhai executor, command policy engine and tool result events. - Closure path: Hook/policy may block one tool, or script observes a result; errors return to original coding context. No separate source-reading auditor returns independent defect judgment as binding corrective work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Custom hooks, a second plan model and tool guards are not automatically complementary audit actors. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/hooks.rs; crates/michin-script/src/hooks.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/commandpolicy.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/tools.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A separately supplied Rhai extension might implement richer audit logic, but its hypothetical behavior cannot be borrowed as default first-party capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment analysis and strategic organizational capability renewal authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long-horizon external requirements and future tool/provider evolution are not addressed by one-session task planning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User selects model/plan track, preserves skills and restores sessions; the single coding agent performs local plans. - Decision owner: No first-party prospective S4 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan and build model tracks, context compaction, persistent session forks, resources and skills. - Closure path: Settings and notes guide later coding turns, but no future-environment options are examined and accepted into organizational capabilities by a strategic owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Memory, switching to a better model and proposing a coding plan are not standalone future-environment intelligence. - Evidence: crates/michin/src/settings.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/agent.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/compact.rs; crates/michin/src/skills.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Maintainer research/roadmap decisions are outside installed runtime ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No constitutive identity/ultimate-purpose policy authority with organizational return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permission risk and destructive commands are local execution safety matters, not highest-order identity/policy conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User configures policies and model mode; runtime follows explicit forbidden-command constraints. - Decision owner: Human operator for task settings and deterministic guard for enforcement; no legitimate highest-level first-party actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Command policy, strict safety flags, Rhai allow/block callbacks and plan-mode mutation gate. - Closure path: Tool permitted/refused or model track changed; no ultimate organizational identity-policy decision returns to regulate the whole operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Constraints and human approvals do not establish an autonomous S5 decision right. - Evidence: crates/michin-agent-core/src/commandpolicy.rs; crates/michin-agent-core/src/hooks.rs; crates/michin/src/config.rs; crates/michin/src/settings.rs. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: OSS repository governance, licensing and developer policy are adjacent to user coding session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5e6d9710b191304c3ba49f7a9abe9f75447e3f50/assessments/michin.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5e6d9710b191304c3ba49f7a9abe9f75447e3f50\">5e6d971 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-19T21:40:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Fennec-AI/agent-runtime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fennec Agent Runtime</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-19\">2026-10-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Fennec-AI/agent-runtime/tree/3441fbde350df50cc070851856c343680babefb4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3441fbde350df50cc070851856c343680babefb4\">3441fbd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Select model-guided actions and tool arguments for an assigned user task, observe their effects or errors and iteratively choose the next action or terminal response. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Novel user instructions, file/tool observations, tool execution errors, rejected permissions, child task results and context-length growth. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ctx.llm model in the shipped query loop emits toolcalls or final text and is fed the actual ToolMessage and child-inbox observations on the next iteration. - Decision owner: Configured model acting through first-party Session/ query and runtools boundary; LangChain/provider services transport inference and host sets tool grants but do not displace model selection of the next substantive task action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party registry and RunContext, safety-filtered tool adapter, BaseChatModel.bindtools, tool executor, child notifications, compaction, cancellation, events, user approval callback and typed terminal result facility. - Closure path: Task → Session.submitmessage / query → llm.bindtools(...).astream → model-selected tool → runtools permission check and tool.ainvoke → ToolMessage in history or child completion notification → same model re-evaluates → terminal result. - Boundary reachability: The public Session facade invokes the shipped query and its tool-dispatch branch; query feeds concrete returned tool results into the next model call and SpawnAgentTool reuses it for children. The README quickstart connects the actual packaged Session, bootstrap, defaulttools() and provider model, not an externally invented loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model, the first-party tool dispatcher and safety infrastructure can execute preselected actions but cannot choose task-sensitive tool calls from new observations. The model owns the material S1 decision, bounded by host-selected tool grants. - Evidence: README.md#L72, agentruntime/session.py#L40, agentruntime/session.py#L89, agentruntime/query.py#L110, agentruntime/query.py#L181, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L102, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L170, agentruntime/tools/spawnagent.py#L83. - Basis: Concrete shipped model-call/tool-output return wiring, not extrapolation from README feature labels alone. - Confidence: High for source-connected operating capability; no local provider inference validation. - Caveats: External LangChain/provider and valid API key/runtime dependencies are needed to execute real tasks; a source-based A does not imply measured reliability or autonomy beyond the configured operating cell.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separate source-backed regulator of specific interference/oscillation between independently viable S1 operating divisions with altered reciprocal operating commitments is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent child tasks may compete for a shared worktree or return conflicting content; async notifications may arrive during parent streaming, but those issues are not the same as interference between independent business S1 systems. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Parent operating model selects subtask calls and may send messages, poll or cancel; runtime has deterministic safe-tool batching and inbox delivery. Neither path senses concrete inter-S1 department conflict, decides dampening, and returns binding mutual adjustment to two viable operating units. - Decision owner: Same parent operating model / host task instructions for delegation, process-local routing and task cancellation code for transport/enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SpawnAgentTool, TaskOutput, TaskStop, SendMessage, AGENTREGISTRY, inbox drain and concurrency semaphore, cooperative cancellation. - Closure path: Parent delegates → child returns or notifies → parent continues current user task; no multi-S1 shared operational interference → distinct coordinating judgment → changed independent operating commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model mediation of child tasks is still the parent S1 operating decision. A messenger, task graph or transport does not automatically confer the S2 function. - Evidence: agentruntime/tools/spawnagent.py#L1, agentruntime/query.py#L143, agentruntime/query.py#L210, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L102, agentruntime/registry.py#L1. - Basis: Source-bounded negative after inspecting real child task, process-inbox and safe-batch modes. - Confidence: Medium-high at the declared one-goal operating recursion. - Caveats: A downstream application could install multiple independently viable S1 agents and new conflict-regulating logic; it would require its own evidence and recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-current management owner of a portfolio of independently viable S1 operations that reviews present obligations/resources and sends binding managerial adjustments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Depth/max-turn constraints, cancellation, shell timeout, active child tasks, approval and context budget threaten single-task execution, but not whole-organizational current performance accountability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User/host configures permission mode, tools, model and maxturns; parent model selects child task instructions, and runtime applies fixed deterministic run limits. No source-backed separate managerial owner negotiates resource allocations or current organizational commitments. - Decision owner: Application operator for delegated bounds and parent operating model for subtask choices; deterministic runtime for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session, RunContext, CONFIG, TaskStop, abort tree, registry, permission callback and structured output checking. - Closure path: Task or tool requests action → configured permission/depth/abort gate applies → same task continues or ends. No whole-current manager&#39;s operating overview → change in S1 portfolio priorities/resources → returned operational adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A class called Session or parent worker does not by itself own discretionary current management of a viable multi-unit organization. - Evidence: agentruntime/session.py#L40, agentruntime/query.py#L102, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L170, agentruntime/cancellation.py#L1, agentruntime/config.py#L1. - Basis: Source-bounded negative for registered source modes, including child and approval routes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Human S3 or autonomous management could exist externally at a higher application boundary; not imported.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary operational-reality inspection of S1/S3 claims with a corrective feedback path to a current S3 executive is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool errors, unsafe actions and malformed typed answers are observable and may be denied or retried. Those observations do not constitute independent challenge of an S3 operating picture. - Decisive decision or feedback right: evaluatepermission may call an external approval callback; hooks log/observe tool use; structured output Pydantic validation checks schema during the same task. No separate first-party S3 correction mandate is wired. - Decision owner: Host approval callback and deterministic tool/typed response checks; not a complementary independent auditor with rights over a current S3 manager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool-level pretool/ posttool/ onerror hooks, permissiondenied events, runstructured, transcript history, registry/cancellation. - Closure path: Tool result or approval decision → returned ToolMessage / validation action / log → current S1 loop; no independent reality sample → corrective S3 audit finding → revised S3 current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permission review and output validation are local action controls, not S3 regardless of their security value. - Evidence: agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L170, agentruntime/permissions.py#L1, agentruntime/hooks.py#L1, agentruntime/session.py#L107. - Basis: Function-first negative: checked concrete validation and hook/approval closure. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A downstream independent evidence checker could leverage the logs, but no such authority is credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No model-owned proactive future environment distinction, prospective organizational adaptation option and adoption back to changed durable future operating capability is evidenced in the shipped default or alternate documented modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long conversations, differing tool results and future continuation after a child completes can require local context adjustment, but not a durable new strategy for a later independent operational cycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autocompactifneeded may ask the configured model to summarize the current history and replace messages at a context threshold; Session persists current messages; TodoWrite updates current task checklist. None owns prospective changes to installed tools, goals, model policies or future agent skill capabilities. - Decision owner: Same operating loop&#39;s compaction / task model and application for any later configuration changes; no distinct S4 adaptation right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compaction/autocompact.py, microcompact.py, token budget, messages and action history, child completion notifications, TODO list tool. - Closure path: Growing current transcript → compact summary in same messages list → same model continues current task; missing prospective future environmental distinction → approved strategy change → reused operational capability loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-written conversation summaries are still local operating-context maintenance, not strategy ownership. No first-party future-option development and adoption is returned into later independent runs. - Evidence: agentruntime/query.py#L74, agentruntime/compaction/autocompact.py#L47, agentruntime/session.py#L89, agentruntime/tools/todowrite.py#L1, README.md#L24. - Basis: Bounded negative after reviewing persistent session/child and compaction modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Host developers could expose a model-written durable skill/update tool, but that would be a new application-owned construction and is not credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate first-party ultimate-purpose/identity decision authority with returned enduring binding organizational policy is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potentially destructive shell/file tools, elevated permission modes and background subagent lifecycle require user approval/limits, but no constitutional identity-level dispute is admitted or decided. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Host user supplies permission mode and optional askcallback; runtime checks allow/deny/ask for tool actions. bypassPermissions is a caller-selected setting, not first-party S5 policy-making authority. - Decision owner: External user/application actor for authorization; deterministic runtime evaluates delegated boundaries and operating model chooses permitted task actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionMode, AskCallback, per-tool allow/deny, tool/child cancellation, logs and optional hooks. - Closure path: Tool requests action → policy gate/host callback → tool executes or denial returned to current agent; no ultimate identity question reaches a legitimate authority and returns as binding constitutional policy governing future task contexts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even a high-friction human gate is not itself a functioning organizational S5 policy/identity constitution. - Evidence: agentruntime/session.py#L40, agentruntime/permissions.py#L1, agentruntime/toolexecutor.py#L170, README.md#L31. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking all named permission modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External human owners retain real final purpose/policy rights; no internally self-deciding S5 organization is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/13dab0d4f2954614f7f99a9fa044ad5b9a154490/assessments/fe/fennec-agent-runtime.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 13dab0d4f2954614f7f99a9fa044ad5b9a154490\">13dab0d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T19:10:06+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bottrall/riffer-rig\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">riffer-rig</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bottrall/riffer-rig/tree/e2e914195f7c15c75ee2ae6bb55d772d754a2f37\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e2e914195f7c15c75ee2ae6bb55d772d754a2f37\">e2e9141 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform coding work through model-selected repository inspection, edits, writes and shell execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar codebases, incomplete task information, file state, failing commands/tests, ambiguous edit targets, model/provider outputs and user corrections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next coding/tool action, interpret tool errors/results and revise subsequent actions until the requested coding outcome is reached. - Decision owner: the active model-backed riffer-rig CodingAgent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CodingAgent, generic-agent execution instantiated by the rig, read/write/edit/bash tools, REPL streaming, runtime cancel flag, hooks/guardrails, AGENTS.md prompt section and provider/model configuration. - Closure path: user/project context → model selects action/tool → tool executes against the working directory → result/error returns to the agent context → model changes subsequent action until completion or cancellation. - Boundary reachability: the installed riffer executable directly instantiates the repository-owned CodingAgent and bundled coding tools; no adjacent development workflow or unconfigured generic runtime feature is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves tools, UI and runtime wrappers but removes the open-ended decisions that turn repository evidence into coding actions. - Evidence: README; lib/riffer/rig/codingagent.rb; lib/riffer/rig/repl.rb; lib/riffer/rig/tools/read.rb; write.rb; edit.rb; bash.rb. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the generic riffer execution kernel is a dependency; only behavior concretely instantiated by the frozen riffer-rig composition is credited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct coordination function among multiple S1 operational units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the focal runtime may execute tools and load extensions/skills, but the standard coding application exposes one model-backed coding unit rather than peer S1 units with an interference relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific choice over inter-S1 conflict, oscillation or mutual adjustment was found. - Decision owner: not established at S2 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime busy state, cancellation, extension ordering and per-runtime working directory. - Closure path: these mechanisms constrain one operational session and do not feed a coordination result back into distinct peer S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: serialization, extension ordering and tool composition do not establish S2 without a concrete inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: README; lib/riffer/rig/runtime.rb; docs/TOOLS.md; docs/SESSIONS.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: embedders can construct multiple runtimes externally, but the frozen first-party rig does not supply a peer-coordination relation among them.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current-control function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cancellation, runtime busy/closed state, tool allowlists and request/tool guardrails constrain current execution within one session. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate owner has a whole-system current view and substantive authority over shared resources, commitments, priorities or interventions across multiple operations. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runtime busy/closed state, cancel flag, request/tool guardrails, hooks, model switching and host notifications. - Closure path: these controls alter or stop the focal session&#39;s current action path but do not form a whole-current metasystemic loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: local execution controls and guardrails are enforcement around S1, not whole-system management. - Evidence: lib/riffer/rig/runtime.rb; runtime/requestguardrail.rb; runtime hooks; REPL model switching. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an embedding application could add fleet management, but such an adjacent composition is outside this concrete coding rig.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool results, command/test failures, hooks and repository tests can reveal mistakes, but they remain ordinary production feedback or adjacent development checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate reviewer/evaluator owns an independent claim-checking judgment that returns findings into a current-control path. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shell/test output, after-tool hooks, notifications and repository test suite. - Closure path: runtime evidence returns to the same coding agent or host; no distinct complementary-access audit loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-checking via bash/tests and hook observation are ordinary operational evidence, not S3. - Evidence: lib/riffer/rig/tools/bash.rb; runtime hooks/events; repository tests. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an extension could implement review behavior, but no such first-party review path is instantiated in the frozen standard rig.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AGENTS.md, user/project skills, provider/model settings and snapshots can change the context used by later turns or restored sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party process senses environmental/future change, generates adaptation options for the rig and returns a selected adaptation into current capability. - Decision owner: not established at S4 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live AGENTS.md loading, filesystem skill backend, model/provider configuration and runtime snapshot/restore. - Closure path: operator-authored instructions/configuration or saved session state are loaded into later operation; no autonomous external-and-prospective adaptation conversation exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: context loading and extensibility reuse externally supplied material but do not autonomously develop future-oriented adaptation options. - Evidence: README; lib/riffer/rig/prompts/agentsmd.rb; lib/riffer/rig/codingagent.rb; docs/SESSIONS.md; settings.rb. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: skills may encode reusable procedures, but this repository does not supply a learning/evolution loop that creates or revises them from environmental evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AGENTS.md instructions, tool allowlists, hooks/guardrails, settings and provider credentials constrain ordinary execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a durable governing decision for the harness. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: instruction hierarchy, request/tool guardrails, tool allowlists, settings and credentials. - Closure path: constraints affect current prompts/tool calls/configuration but do not close identity-level policy questions for subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static/configurable prompts and guardrails are execution policy mechanisms, not S5. - Evidence: prompts/agentsmd.rb; runtime/requestguardrail.rb; runtime.rb; settings.rb; README. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project maintainers and users can change instructions/configuration outside the run, but that adjacent authority is not a first-party S5 runtime loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/03ba69b67f5d1b4947c68a70e1515f45faaa64f1/assessments/riffer-rig.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 03ba69b67f5d1b4947c68a70e1515f45faaa64f1\">03ba69b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-21T16:49:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/markmhendrickson/ateles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ateles</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-21\">2026-10-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/markmhendrickson/ateles/tree/516edbf0bf6a96654f69b8a797fd2998aa4983cb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 516edbf0bf6a96654f69b8a797fd2998aa4983cb\">516edbf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Agent work cells perform model-guided coding, reviews, release handling, mail/calendar/content tasks in the configured personal swarm. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended task requirements and changing tool/work results, including code defects, review findings and external requests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The loaded agent/skill chooses task-semantic tool actions and adapts during a headless Codex/Claude/Cursor execution; specific risky outward effects are separately gated. - Decision owner: Each dispatched, role-defined model agent within an Ateles-controlled T4 invocation; external model inference alone is not assigned supervisory authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Neotoma agentdefinition mirror/loader, signed grants, provider selector, context/system prompt, controlled subprocess, task/session records, event dispatch. - Closure path: Event/task arrives → Apis/Anthus selects role and builds scoped prompt → first-party runner launches model tool loop → result/agent-produced outputs are recorded to Neotoma, can enter follow-up workflow and approved external action. - Boundary reachability: execution/daemons/apis/apis.py::spawnharnessskill() calls shipped skillrunner.runskill(); runner constructs actual CLI commands and subprocesses for named agents, and anthus.py::spawnagent() provides another wired route. External inference is a configured dependency for shipped first-party autonomous work cell, not a separately administered employee. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The roles&#39; model-driven semantic choices and tool paths are bounded by first-party orchestration, identity and task/result recording. Removing the model leaves deterministic dispatch but no S1 problem-solving; keeping it in this shipped path realizes S1. - Evidence: api; runner; anthus; prereq; readme - Basis: structural and explicit -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent damaging overlap between distinct S1 agent builds targeting the same repository issue and return deferred work into task execution when the collision ceases. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independent dispatched work cells can act on the same repository#issue before the first completes; uncoordinated duplicate editing/PR delivery would cause interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select which in-flight build retains the issue key, hold the overlapping task with an exact deferral record, and release/re-dispatch eligible deferred work when the claim ends. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party Apis/builddelivery machinery owns atomic in-process claim and recovery; task agents execute after the coordination decision, but there is no demonstrated agent discretion in choosing the coordination policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: activebuild, claimbuild, deferralcontext, guarded Neotoma task writes, release hook, startup sweep, deferred task recovery and scoped worktree. - Closure path: A second task finds an active issue claim and becomes a durable deferredbehindbuild task; the original holder&#39;s release signals a recovery sweep, which checks fresh state and re-dispatches only qualified work. Different task agents then resume in the permitted order. - Boundary reachability: apis.py&#39;s task dispatcher and recovery paths use builddelivery and skillrunner.runskill; these are shipped Python modules, not foundation-only future requirements. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The code establishes a concrete inter-S1 collision and specific attenuation with feedback; C recognizes this first-party function-specific coordination construction without pretending the model agent chooses the rules. - Evidence: delivery; api; runner - Basis: structural - Confidence: moderate-high - Caveats: The in-flight claim is explicitly process-local, not a distributed lease among independently deployed operator instances. Broader design claims are not inherited.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: ? - Function: Whole-current swarm regulation over task priorities, resource limits, external-effect commitments and corrective intervention is plausible but not fully evidenced as one organization-level S3 decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Cross-domain execution costs, asynchronous task backlog, delivery collisions and approval risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A whole-system authority should choose/revise operational commitments and priorities using a global view and return changes to S1 agent work. - Decision owner: The operator controls some execution policy, while deterministic dispatch gates constrain tasks; the evidence does not yet establish a single decisive current-control owner and closed whole-system feedback path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Blast/confidence gate, role grants, configurable model tier, task queue, checkpoints, provider capacity windows and reports. - Closure path: Per-action approval/checkpoint and per-task retry are implemented, but a sourced whole-swarm performance/resource bargaining and accountable reallocation loop was not reconstructed. - Boundary reachability: No positive S3 published; the first-party task gate and current-task control paths remain plausible and prevent a bounded —. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A threshold gate and routing/scheduling are not by themselves global resource regulation; the need to inspect live, accepted priorities and operator override return paths is material. - Evidence: policy; api; arch; status - Basis: structural with unresolved semantics -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate complementary, sufficiently independent audit judgment with findings returned into a current S3 management decision is established for the shipped swarm. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Untruthful task completion, inaccurate recorded evidence or mistaken routine checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A distinct, independent access path must challenge normal S1/S3 reporting and feed corrective control. - Decision owner: Routine reviewers, workflow sign-offs, CI checks and Neotoma audit records exist; no function-specific complementary auditor/decisive feedback owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Signed identities, observation provenance, developer/tests, review gates, operator checkpoints, archive and drift records. - Closure path: Ordinary review/gate findings can block one task, but a distinct sporadic audit channel + corrective whole-system management return is not shown. - Boundary reachability: No positive state claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate reviewer role, signature or audit trail alone cannot establish S3 at the assessed agent-organization recursion. - Evidence: status; gates; arch; general - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Convert externally relevant runtime/organizational drift and contradictions into prospective policy-adjustment options for later authorization and operational adoption. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New conditions, recurring failures, tool/provider behaviours, misfit of agent operating rules against task environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Distinguish repeated environmental drift, propose changed agent policy, and present an adaptation option to governing authority for incorporation into future operating rules. - Decision owner: The first-party generalizer clusters drift and creates signed agentpolicy/revision proposals; ultimate active-policy authorization is left to the operator/approval path, with no self-promotion of live rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Anthus drift harvesting, threshold/risk classification, operator-conflict detection, signed proposal write, Neotoma-backed policy reader. - Closure path: anthus harvests drift → generalizer.processsignals classifies clusters and persists policy/revision proposals → separately authorized operator must approve/adopt → live agentpolicy loader/governing runtime can change subsequent behaviour. Last integration is a configured approval/composition path, not proven autonomous promotion. - Boundary reachability: lib/daemonruntime/generalizer.py::actoncluster emits first-party rule proposals from harvested signals; execution/daemons/anthus/anthus.py invokes harvesting, and loader/gate read current policies. This is a prospective adaptation constructor path within the shipped system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A concrete option generator and policy return surface go beyond internal task planning; C, not A, because decisions on cross-cutting rule adoption remain operator-gated. - Evidence: general; anthus; agentload; policy - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Maintain the single-operator swarm&#39;s legitimate organizational policy and identity boundary under a named ultimate human principal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Cross-agent role authority, rules for what may be done autonomously, and conflicts between proposed policy drift and operator-owned governing commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Approve or reject changes to ultimate agent policies, grant scope and identity/action authority, which later runtime decisions must obey. - Decision owner: One explicitly documented founder/operator principal governs the single-operator reference. Agent-generated rule proposals cannot overwrite operator-authored policies; multi-operator ownership/quorum remains design-only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Neotoma-canonical agentpolicy, signed agent-to-principal identity and grants, operator-only blast class and runtime fail-closed policy reader, change proposals. - Closure path: Agent observes/presents proposed policy/authority revision → legitimate operator approves/adopts into canonical Neotoma policy → runtime loads resulting approved policy and applies it to subsequent agent dispatch/action gating. Human approval of individual routine PRs alone is NOT the S5 witness. - Boundary reachability: The repo ships the single-principal regime and reads effective policies in gating.loadpolicy/agent loader; generalizer explicitly defers operator-conflicting and high-layer changes to the parent. The authority binding is part of the supported P1 reference, not the hypothetical P2–P5 distributed system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent may propose; ultimate identity/policy remains with the human principal and the authorized version returns into action constraints. No autonomous S5 mode is claimed. - Evidence: readme; policy; general; agentload; status - Basis: explicit and structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/907b625f88397c09eddfe65ec1374701d39e117d/assessments/at/ateles.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 907b625f88397c09eddfe65ec1374701d39e117d\">907b625 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T16:19:17Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gagans23/agent-os\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-os</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gagans23/agent-os/tree/d318787db1fce4163e470ac1c94f5a74d667303f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d318787db1fce4163e470ac1c94f5a74d667303f\">d318787 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform user-submitted personal-agent tasks into model-generated operational results under persistent context and reusable skills. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user objectives, changing remembered/contextual information, matched skill procedures, provider responses, task failures and task-specific observations captured in the job trajectory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: generate the substantive task response/action content from the current command plus runtime-supplied memory/skill context. - Decision owner: the configured autonomous model actor reached through the first-party provider-backed agentfn mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CommandRouter, runjob(), provider adapters, persistent memory/jobs, hooks, skill matching, traces, risk classification and configured provider transport. - Closure path: command → router/risk admission → memory and matched-skill context → provider-backed autonomous agent function → result/trace persistence → returned user result and later memory/learning availability. - Boundary reachability: a configured provider is an explicitly supported standard operating mode; CommandRouter automatically converts it to agentfn and /run calls the first-party runjob() path. No adjacent development/evaluation agent is needed to supply the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: first-party Python code transports context and invokes the model, but the substantive response for an admitted task is selected by the autonomous model actor rather than predetermined by the router. The model service itself remains an external dependency, as with other model-backed harnesses. - Evidence: pinned agentos/providers.py, agentos/commandrouter.py, agentos/runner.py and README provider/run documentation. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: with no provider configured agent-os can remain in deterministic fallback mode; A records the supported first-party model-backed operating mode, not a claim that every configuration is agent-owned.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: suppress a concrete shared-state collision among simultaneously executing swarm sub-jobs so sibling operations can proceed without corrupting or blocking one another during shared-store initialization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent workers opening the shared jobs/memory SQLite stores can race on the journal-mode/WAL transition and produce database is locked failures before useful parallel work begins. - Decisive decision or feedback right: establish the shared database schema/WAL state once before fan-out rather than letting sibling workers race to establish it independently. - Decision owner: the first-party deterministic Orchestrator implementation/developer-selected coordination policy; no autonomous agent is shown choosing or revising this conflict-response rule at runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: initshareddb(), SQLite WAL/busy-timeout behavior, ThreadPoolExecutor, per-worker JobStore/AgentMemory connections and the normal swarm execution path. - Closure path: swarm admitted → initshareddb() initializes the shared stores → parallel worker connections open against already-prepared stores → sibling sub-jobs execute and return results instead of colliding on journal initialization. - Boundary reachability: initshareddb() is wired directly and unconditionally into first-party Orchestrator.run() before ThreadPoolExecutor fan-out in the shipped /swarm path; no downstream composition is needed to expose the coordination primitive. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the S2 function and feedback path are concrete, but the decisive response to the inter-worker conflict is a fixed runtime policy. The model may choose sub-tasks, but it does not own this coordination discretion, so the autonomous A threshold is not met. - Evidence: pinned agentos/orchestrator.py, especially initshareddb() and the explicit implementation comment explaining the database is locked race it prevents. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this classification is not based on decomposition, parallelism or synthesis by themselves; it is based on the specific inter-S1 shared-store collision and the first-party attenuation path. C records a real S2-specific first-party path without autonomous ownership of the decisive coordination rule. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently executing swarm sub-jobs, each performing a separate user-derived operational task through runjob() and producing its own result. - Inter-S1 disturbance: sibling workers contend on common jobs/memory SQLite initialization and can race on journal-mode switching, yielding database is locked. - Attenuating coordination relation: the orchestrator initializes/closes the common stores once before worker fan-out, leaving workers to open already-prepared WAL/schema state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: after pre-initialization the sibling workers can enter their normal job execution paths concurrently rather than fail at shared-store setup. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the source explicitly ties the pre-fan-out relation to a concrete concurrency collision among sibling workers; it is not credited merely because a queue, shared database or parallel executor exists.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system current-control function was established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current job health, pending approvals, process failures, recent scores and swarm execution are observable or locally regulated, but no whole-installation resource/commitment/prioritization disturbance is shown under a qualifying S3 owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no actor with a whole-system current view and discretionary authority over shared current resources, commitments, priorities, constraints, accountability or synergy was established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /status, /health, job statistics, risk gates, human approvals for individual privileged commands, fixed supervisor restart/backoff rules, swarm concurrency configuration and persistent job state. - Closure path: no qualifying whole-system current-control decision→subsequent-operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the available machinery observes or enforces local/static decisions. A human can approve a particular privileged action, but that is not evidence of whole-system S3 parent governance. - Evidence: pinned agentos/commandrouter.py, approvals.py, supervisor.py, orchestrator.py, jobs/health surfaces and README governance documentation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: later versions or downstream operator organizations could add S3 without changing the meaning of this frozen assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no qualifying first-party complementary and sufficiently independent audit function was established inside agent-os. - Disturbance / variety regulated: traces, commands, approval decisions, scores and daily reliability summaries are recorded/checked, but they do not establish a separate first-party challenge channel to operational reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party auditor with materially independent access and a findings→control return path was found. - Decision owner: not established inside the assessed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trace recording, hash-chained audit log and verify(), /audit, daily evaluation summaries, Ninja Harness invocation and persisted external scores. - Closure path: no qualifying first-party complementary audit finding is returned into current control as an independently owned S3 judgment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit database verifies integrity of the same recorded command/decision path; it does not independently challenge operational claims. Ninja Harness can supply a distinct evaluation judgment, but that judgment is owned by a separately scoped external system and is not imported into agent-os ownership. - Evidence: pinned agentos/audit.py, runner.py, dailyeval.py, README&#39;s explicit runtime/evaluation separation and batch-#89 boundary note. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external evaluation may still be operationally valuable and may feed adaptation proposals; the standalone VSM ownership boundary is narrower than an integrated multi-system deployment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: turn a demonstrated novel user workflow into a reusable future capability when a legitimate parent human accepts the proposed adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: successful external/user work can reveal recurring procedures not represented in the current skill library, creating future capability mismatch if the runtime cannot preserve and reuse the newly demonstrated workflow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether the proposed reusable SKILL.md becomes an installed capability for future agent runs. - Decision owner: the human/operator parent through the explicit /approve or /reject gate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trace capture, success/novelty/complexity tests in proposeskill(), draft persistence, approval queue, skill-file write, SkillRegistry.reload() and later skill matching in runjob(). - Closure path: novel successful user task → first-party trace-derived skill draft → human review/approval → skill written into the live library and registry reloaded → later matching task receives the reusable procedure in its execution context. - Boundary reachability: the learning path is wired into ordinary first-party /run/approved-run completion through maybeproposeskill() and the same standard approval command surface; no custom extension or repository-development actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent-os autonomously constructs the candidate adaptation, but maintainer code explicitly refuses to install it silently. The decisive capability-change right belongs to the parent human, so the published state is standalone P, not A. - Evidence: pinned agentos/skillsynth.py, agentos/commandrouter.py, agentos/skillregistry.py, approvals.py, runner.py and README learning-loop documentation. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the separate weak-run ImprovementProposal path is propose-only and requires an additional apply step; it is supporting evidence of conservative adaptation design, not an autonomous S4 closure. External Ninja scores may contribute a signal in some learning paths but do not own agent-os&#39;s parent adaptation decision. - External distinction: a real user/environment task succeeds through a procedure not already covered by the installed skill set; the learned option is grounded in the task&#39;s recorded operational trace rather than generated from an internal roadmap alone. - Future / prospective distinction: the draft is created specifically as a reusable skill so later matching work can exploit the demonstrated procedure instead of rediscovering it. - Adaptation option generated: a concrete SKILL.md draft containing name, triggers, reconstructed procedure and verification instructions. - Path back into current capability / S3: parent approval writes the draft to the active skills directory, reloads the registry, and subsequent runjob() calls can match/inject the new procedure into model context.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the declared installation recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider choice, risk rules, profiles, packs, skills and per-action approvals constrain behavior, but no evidenced identity/ultimate-policy dispute is escalated to and closed by a legitimate S5 authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying ultimate identity/policy decision path was established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured profiles/tool allowlists, pluggable risk classifier, human approval of privileged actions, provider configuration, role packs and static governance defaults. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → authority → authoritative decision → returned governing operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts/configuration and human vetoes over ordinary actions constrain S1 work but do not constitute S5 under the Profile without an identity-level issue and ultimate-policy closure. - Evidence: pinned agentos/profiles.py, risk.py, approvals.py, provider/pack configuration, command router and README governance material. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an operator obviously retains ordinary ownership of a self-hosted installation; the Methodology does not infer S5=P from generic configuration authority or per-task approvals.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7d01d7323bb17fab796dd0efd2045bbfa1075563/assessments/gagans-agent-os.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7d01d7323bb17fab796dd0efd2045bbfa1075563\">7d01d73 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:27:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aohp-os/aohp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AOHP</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aohp-os/aohp/tree/b8ab9e98a2bd51f7fbd01a95d3b45dca078c5884\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b8ab9e98a2bd51f7fbd01a95d3b45dca078c5884\">b8ab9e9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: enact user-requested service outcomes through model-driven agents operating across application/system capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous apps/interfaces, user intent, GUI/API/CLI state, cross-app information and task-specific environmental changes during execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the agent actor chooses task actions/service-composition steps under the user&#39;s intent. - Decision owner: autonomous AI agent actor hosted by the AOHP harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OS APIs/CLIs/GUI access, background execution, memory, sandboxed sensitive values and information-flow tracking. - Closure path: agent choices invoke OS/application actions, observations return through the harness and subsequent agent behaviour responds until the requested service outcome is produced. - Why agent-owned: AOHP explicitly places agents as first-class operating actors; deterministic OS mechanisms execute/constrain rather than replace the agent&#39;s task decisions. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: —. - AOHP supports parallel background interaction and shared OS resources, but the pinned repository evidence does not identify distinct S1 units plus a specific inter-S1 interference/oscillation and a corresponding coordination feedback path. OS scheduling, sandboxing and information-flow enforcement are not promoted merely because they can constrain concurrent actors. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - The OS can enforce permissions, information-flow constraints, background execution and user-consent gates, but no distinct first-party actor is evidenced with a whole-system current view plus discretionary authority over shared operational resources, commitments or priorities. - Sensitive-transfer/payment consent is an action-level authorization mechanism; it does not establish a whole-system S3 parent loop. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: —. - Security-oriented test cases and information-flow metadata validate/enforce the product&#39;s security model, but no independent runtime audit actor/path is shown challenging ordinary S1/S3 reporting and returning corrective findings into current control. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Personalization and cross-app memory can improve task context, but the pinned boundary does not establish an externally and prospectively oriented intelligence loop that models future environmental change, develops adaptation options and returns them into current organizational capability. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - User intent and consent are important authority inputs, and AOHP&#39;s security policies constrain execution. However, the cited paths concern task/sensitive-action authorization rather than an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue reaching legitimate parent authority and returning to govern the organization as a whole. - Static/security enforcement is therefore not promoted to S5 or P. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f/assessments/aohp.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f\">4155c43 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T22:59:46+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zwpdbh/octopus-in-rust\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Octopus</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zwpdbh/octopus-in-rust/tree/88695f8e9209c76522d5943a2207751fd382dd43\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 88695f8e9209c76522d5943a2207751fd382dd43\">88695f8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: pursue user-specified coding outcomes and focused subagent investigations/edits using model-chosen tools and first-party execution-feedback loops. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository uncertainty, failing tools, changing source/workspace state, subtask results, interruptions, provider and permission errors, and needed plan adjustments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root or child model selects the next code-reading, editing, command, plan, search or delegated task and adapts after actual returned result. - Decision owner: first-party octopus-cli KimiSoul model and child Agent sessions; the external model provider performs inference but native Brain and agent tools own the workflow and action routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent-core streaming Brain, KimiSoul toolset, tool registry, prompt profiles, hooks, permissions, checkpoints, background task manager and child status store. - Closure path: user task → native coding-agent model/tool call → approval and real tool/subagent execution → observation or child report → same model&#39;s next coding decision or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: apps/octopus-cli/src/agents/default/agent.yaml registers the native Agent tool; apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/agent/mod.rs creates and runs named child sessions with own model/tool loop, while crates/agent-core/src/core/brain.rs implements executable streamed tool/result turns for the native root runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without model-selected actions and interpretation of tool/subagent outcomes, file/LLM transports and status stores cannot choose what work to do next. - Evidence: apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/agent.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/kimisoul.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/brainbridge.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/agent/mod.rs; crates/agent-core/src/core/brain.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/agents/default/agent.yaml. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this demonstrates first-party operational model rights, not success in any live programming benchmark or all model settings.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no implemented native inter-S1 interference/oscillation attenuation relation is demonstrated for the concurrently runnable coding subagents at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overlapping parent/child or child/child actions could contend for the same files, task responsibility or tool permissions; the source provides child launch/status and user-approved tool execution but not collision-specific conflict detection and feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no established native mutually-exclusive work claim, inter-agent dependency arbitration, shared write conflict adjudication or returned attenuation to conflicting S1s. Approval is about user permission for each requested tool action, not collision between peer commitments. - Decision owner: root model for delegation and standard permission/runtime for operation gating; no observed distinct S2 coordination decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LaborMarket for task role selection, SubagentStore for running/completed/error status, shared approval callback with source IDs, background shell task controls and notification broker. - Closure path: a delegated child runs its own task and may complete asynchronously; shared registry and approvals track each task, but no conflict-specific outcome is returned to multiple S1s to attenuate their interference. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concurrent spawning and monitoring is not itself collision arbitration; agent roles, status messages, and one approval queue do not demonstrate cross-agent conflict regulation. - Evidence: apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/agent/mod.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/subagents/mod.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/approvalruntime/runtime.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/background/manager.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/background/mod.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/notifications/manager.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: native background child tasks can genuinely run in parallel; their concurrency is acknowledged rather than denied. The negative is the absence of a demonstrated collision-specific coordination relation, not absence of multiple active agents.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-current organization management owner with an evidenced global resource/commitment judgment returned to several simultaneously operating S1s was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task decomposition, background children and tool failures are handled as the root coding request. A list of running subagents exists but no first-party portfolio status→reassignment/control loop spanning current commitments was demonstrated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root chooses to spawn a requested child, tool approvals constrain execution and subagent records can be queried in code; neither establishes whole-current reprioritization of a team/work portfolio. - Decision owner: root coding agent for task delegation and human user for tool consent, not an evidenced S3 controller of all current operative units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed Agent tool and task/result store, per-child approval source and session, local background task manager, notifications, prompt role policies and UI event streams. - Closure path: child tool results may return to the root for further coding, or background completion is recorded/logged; no whole-current status view with an owned global management decision and returned commitment change to multiple active workers is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing individual delegates and checking status is operational orchestration for the current request. A native parent model would need a demonstrated distinct global/current management right and closed corrective return to justify S3, not just tool access to async children. - Evidence: apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/agent/mod.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/subagents/mod.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/agent.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/notifications/manager.rs; crates/agent-core/src/control.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: other standalone bot/web service deployments may have their own operational oversight; those different recursions are not imported as terminal coding S3 owners.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party complementary audit with sufficiently independent raw-evidence access, separate judgment and consequential return to present organizational control was demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code may be wrong, shell may fail, model summaries may be overconfident; standard tool results, build CI or self-requested exploration may expose errors but are routine task feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate audit selector/mandate distinct from the producing model and current task role that probes alternative primary evidence and makes an accountable audit judgment for current management. - Decision owner: root or child executing tasks, user for approval and maintainer CI for external development checks; no native S3 auditor/manager identified at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: file/command result logs, hooks on tool use/failure, status notifications, explore role with read-only prompt policy, checkpoint/rollback DMail, developer workflows. - Closure path: tool outcomes, hook events and reports return to the same task loop or user; no independent adversarial audit → findings → distinct current control decision → corrected operational follow-up is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model with read-only tools or separate session can perform code search, but a generic explore subtask and hook are not independently commissioned risk-based audit/alternate truth with returned S3 authority. - Evidence: apps/octopus-cli/src/agents/default/agent.yaml; apps/octopus-cli/src/agents/default/explore.yaml; apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/agent/mod.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/hooks/engine.rs; crates/agent-core/src/hooks/policy.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/brainbridge.rs; .github/workflows/ci.yml. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user might compose an external auditor using generic tools, but no specific shipped independent complementary audit mode is shown here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective external environment modelling with adaptation options returned into present managerial capability choices was established at the coding-agent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider errors, new user tasks, changing source files, plugin availability, background task events and prompt context are operational disturbances, not forecast-driven future capability management. - Decisive decision or feedback right: runtime retries/recovery rules and model-guided coding/plans govern current operations; no future-facing option generator and S4→S3 returned tradeoff decision is implemented. - Decision owner: current operative agent for task steps; developer/user for plugin/provider/tool configurations, not an evidenced native S4 intelligence authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: retry/backoff, recovery policy, compaction, prompt injection, plugin discovery, session state and task planning. - Closure path: new tool or source information informs current model context and retries; no externally prospective sensing → tested adaptation alternatives → current organization management conversation → new capability commitment cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory, planning, checkpoint rollback and newly available plugins aid current task execution but do not meet the future-oriented organizational adaptation criterion. - Evidence: crates/agent-core/src/core/retry.rs; crates/agent-core/src/core/recovery.rs; crates/agent-core/src/session/compaction.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/dynamicinjection.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/plugin/discovery.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/tools/plan/mod.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the same monorepo hosts external-facing applications with their own changing market/user contexts; they are separate from the specified installed coding agent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party highest-level identity/ultimate-policy decision authority and returned binding governance path is established for this terminal coding agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool execution safety, human approvals, read-only exploration, model settings and plugin permissions concern immediate technical boundaries, not competing identity/mission claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users approve/deny particular operations, runtime applies approved/denied tool modes; maintainer AGENTS.md defines contribution rules but not executable legitimate first-party ultimate-policy arbitration. - Decision owner: operator for local consent and developer for static tool policies, not a verified S5 actor resolving current/future whole-system identity conflicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool approval, per-subagent approval source/queue, default role tool policy, security hooks and provider configurations. - Closure path: unsafe tool request is denied or allowed, which affects the current task; no durable supreme identity/ethos decision returns to S3/S4 governance of subsequent operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary human permission decisions or source policy text do not create a genuine highest-authority organizational policy loop. - Evidence: apps/octopus-cli/src/approvalruntime/runtime.rs; crates/agent-core/src/core/approval.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/soul/approval.rs; apps/octopus-cli/src/agents/default/agent.yaml; AGENTS.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: user organization, project maintainers or app-specific human policies may exercise legitimate governance externally, not as first-party native S5 for this coding agent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3f308b59dc96279cae7272e505c0bd705aec00ec/assessments/octopus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3f308b59dc96279cae7272e505c0bd705aec00ec\">3f308b5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:27:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/alibaba/open-code-review\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCodeReview</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/alibaba/open-code-review/tree/7a571b78d3493b249f6ad14d835c6a79a0a67d2e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7a571b78d3493b249f6ad14d835c6a79a0a67d2e\">7a571b7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: inspect assigned code/diff material and produce substantive review findings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: language/framework variation, repository context, defect/risk ambiguity, cross-file evidence and changing source content. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the review agent decides which potential issues warrant comments and which context/tools to use before finishing its subtask. - Decision owner: model-driven OCR-managed review agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic file selection, bundling, rules, tool registry, line anchoring and comment worker pool. - Closure path: agent inspection/tool results change subsequent reasoning; accepted codecomment results become the delivered review output. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: —. - Multiple file-group subagents can run with isolated contexts and concurrency, but the pinned evidence does not establish a specific mutual interference/conflict/oscillation among operational S1 units plus a feedback relation that regulates it. Divide-and-conquer bundling and asynchronous scheduling alone are not S2. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - Deterministic orchestration controls selection, grouping, rule application and scheduling, but no distinct autonomous actor has both a current whole-review view and discretionary authority over shared organizational resources, commitments or priorities. Pipeline control is not promoted to S3. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: —. - The reflection/positioning machinery is separate in implementation but runs as part of the ordinary codecomment production/post-processing path. The reviewed evidence does not reconstruct a sufficiently independent complementary access path that challenges normal operational reporting and returns findings to a distinct current-control owner. - Put differently, “independent module” is not sufficient by itself: reflection here is routine quality processing of the review product, and no separate corrective organizational loop is established at this boundary. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Repository search, dynamic context retrieval and production-derived tool tuning improve review execution but do not form an external-and-prospective environmental intelligence loop with adaptation options returned into current organizational capability. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - Review rules, configured models/providers and operator-selected execution modes constrain the product, but no runtime identity/ultimate-policy authority and return loop is established. Human consumption/disposition of review comments is outside the supplied harness governance path. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f/assessments/open-code-review.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f\">4155c43 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T13:42:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/p0systems/peezy-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Peezy</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/p0systems/peezy-cli/tree/4abb65bb3e8c9f6c4cdd42c22d29b61531bffad3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4abb65bb3e8c9f6c4cdd42c22d29b61531bffad3\">4abb65b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify and verify a software project using model-selected coding/tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar repository state, implementation choices, tool/process failures, context pressure, provider behavior and denied actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, interpret their outcomes, adapt after failures/denials and decide when the task is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Peezy agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry, gateway streaming, approvals, path confinement, todo state, compaction, session persistence/resume, MCP registration and interrupt handling. - Closure path: user objective → model selects tool/coding action → runtime executes or returns denial/error → result re-enters model history → model revises work → final answer closes the turn. - Boundary reachability: both interactive and headless entry points construct the same first-party Agent and tool registry; headless changes approval handling without replacing the core decision loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code enforces permissions and transports tool results, but the model owns the open-ended coding decisions and evidence interpretation. - Evidence: README.md; src/core/agent.ts; src/core/create-agent.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ask/plan modes can constrain execution, but that does not transfer the coding decision itself away from the model.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material inter-S1 coordination function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: none at the declared recursion because the standard runtime does not instantiate distinct concurrent operational agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: todo sequencing, MCP tool registration and tool execution order remain inside one S1 loop. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic task ordering/tool extensibility is not peer interference attenuation. - Evidence: src/core/agent.ts; src/core/tools/todo.ts; src/core/mcp/manager.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: connected MCP servers may themselves contain agents, but external systems cannot donate S2 to Peezy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system current-control function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no portfolio of distinct current operational commitments exists under a separate controller. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval prompts, request caps, abort signals, plan-mode mutation rejection and todo visibility constrain one S1 turn. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human approval is action-local permission and interrupt is termination enforcement; neither provides a whole-system current view plus substantive control over resources/commitments. - Evidence: src/core/agent.ts; src/core/tools/safety.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic approval or stop controls are explicitly insufficient for S3 under Methodology 0.3.6.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material independent complementary audit path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no separate actor independently challenges an operational claim using a complementary evidence path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the same coding agent may run tests, inspect diffs or use read tools; plan mode and todo tracking do not create independent review. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no fresh reviewer/verifier role or separate evidence channel is wired into the standard distribution. - Evidence: src/core/prompt/system.ts; src/core/agent.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can ask the same agent or an external MCP tool to review; generic composition is not a first-party independent audit loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no future/external distinction is transformed into an adaptation option that changes current organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session resume, compaction, provider/model configuration, live MCP changes and project instructions preserve/reconfigure context but do not themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: compaction summarizes prior context and configuration selects tools/models; neither is an autonomous prospective adaptation owner. - Evidence: src/core/prompt/compaction.ts; src/core/session/store.ts; src/core/create-agent.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: persistent experience can inform later S1 turns, but persistence/context reuse is insufficient S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level policy matter is routed to an authoritative owner and returned as governing runtime policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compiled system prompt, approval modes, path confinement, project instructions and provider/tool configuration constrain operations. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Peezy operates within user/developer-authored rules and has no authority to redefine its identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: src/core/prompt/system.ts; src/core/agent.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: action approvals are not identity governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1990b5aa53ad357c8c87753b6ceaccd697711001/assessments/peezy.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1990b5aa53ad357c8c87753b6ceaccd697711001\">1990b5a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T21:55:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zap-coding-agent/zap-coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Zap</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zap-coding-agent/zap-coding-agent/tree/9812a142b1d12c894c98a6f5d08cc776ecc760da\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9812a142b1d12c894c98a6f5d08cc776ecc760da\">9812a14 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: choose and carry out codebase engineering work under uncertainty, including source inspection, modifications, shell/test execution and error-driven repairs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown program structure and test behavior, failed commands, tool limitations, insufficient context and unexpected code interactions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model selects the next specific tool/argument or correction after observing actual results; invoked child runs its own independent model-tool loop. - Decision owner: active coding model in Session (or in a delegated child), not shell/tool dispatcher, task-index or watchdog. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LLM client, session context and turn loop, code index, file/shell tools, permission gate, turn and verification budgets. - Closure path: user goal → model-selected read/edit/execute action → source or shell result → next model turn or completion; tool failure may trigger model-directed revision. - Boundary reachability: normal CLI/TUI session and first-party spawnagent registered in the default non-child session runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the same deterministic tools alone cannot invent appropriate engineering actions without a model decision. - Evidence: README.md; ARCHITECTURE.md; turn.rs; tools.rs; mod.rs; agent.rs; agentcore.rs - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no specific observed or source-wired first-party attenuation of material inter-S1 interference with returned behavior change is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent write-capable delegated children can collide in a shared working tree; duplicative work and resource contention are plausible inter-worker disturbances. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no inspected controller detects such a concrete collision and limits or reallocates participating child work; source tool&#39;s filesinscope is not consumed by the dispatch path. - Decision owner: parent model verbally instructed to split independent work, with deterministic tool batching; neither is an executed S2 conflict resolution owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnagent prompts, optional filesinscope declaration, per-child session isolation, tool call parallelism, user-operated background session tracker. - Closure path: child task may return a result to the parent; no independently evidenced conflict report → attenuation affecting the conflicting children → updated child work closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a concurrent spawnagent response and “do not edit same file” instruction do not detect and settle actual inter-S1 interference, and UI background status is not a corrective channel. - Evidence: agent.rs; agentcore.rs; tools.rs; backgroundagent.rs; mod.rs; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural and explicit execute argument usage -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separately evidenced current whole-organization managerial decision for the live roster/commitments of subordinate coding S1s. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failing delegated work, conflicting team priorities and resource/commitment tradeoffs of several simultaneous coding workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent can issue additional isolated child subtasks and consume their final answers; no built-in whole-current roster view and discretionary active priority reassignment applied to existing model-running child tasks was found. - Decision owner: parent coding model controls its present task decomposition, while /bg list/start/kill remains a user operation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-session task lists, user background agent status panel, automatic model routing by task classifier, plan step runner and tool watchdog. - Closure path: delegated output returns to parent coding turn, and user background status to UI; no documented active whole-team status → discretionary control choice → changed current commitments across children and closed return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: decomposition of one coding request into independent calls, or one process&#39;s retries/verification, is S1 strategy and task execution rather than whole-current organizational control. - Evidence: agent.rs; backgroundagent.rs; mod.rs; taskplanner.rs; planexecution.rs; watchdog.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separately owned independent source-first auditor forms a corrective challenge to a coding worker&#39;s claims and returns to a distinct current-control manager within the shipped organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a coding agent&#39;s potentially false edit/test-success claims, missed regressions or unsafe modifications. - Decisive decision or feedback right: watchdog detects repeated failing verification commands, and plans require tests, but the same coding loop acts on those outputs; no separately commissioned independent auditor verdict and managerial corrective return is wired as a first-party mode. - Decision owner: normal code verification remains with originating coding model and deterministic watchdog; human/CI reviewers are outside runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: watchdog.rs per-command fail ledger/nudges and tool withdrawal, planexecution.rs step verification rules, shell output, user-visible audit log. - Closure path: test failure returns to the same coding actor and can alter its next step or cause stop; no independent reviewer → distinct manager → affected coder corrective cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: watchdog and editing-plan QA are task execution reliability protections, not separate complementary audit ownership. - Evidence: watchdog.rs; planexecution.rs; tools.rs; turn.rs; agent.rs; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no agentic outside/future-oriented organizational capability strategy plus applied capacity adaptation cycle. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing coding requirements, future language support, provider/skill constraints and environment capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate actor selects long-horizon capability changes from external signals and implements them into the agent organization. - Decision owner: developer/operator configures provider, skill/index settings and deployments; model addresses the current coding task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AST code-index, dynamic skill matching and context refresh, task planning, persistence and provider routing. - Closure path: contexts or skills inform current action, but no future organizational-capability choice → decision → implementation → feedback loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adaptive context retrieval and task-level planning are not equivalent to future-capacity intelligence. - Evidence: mod.rs; skillmanager.rs; memoryrefresh.rs; taskplanner.rs; mod.rs; contextmanager.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate-purpose/policy authority over the agent organization is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mission/identity tradeoffs distinct from per-command safety decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S5 owner arbitrates organizational purpose and returns binding governing change to multiple S1s. - Decision owner: user/operator and developer configuration set purpose/permissions; built-in permission gate and watchdog enforce narrower runtime safety conditions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permissions, destructive-command checks, sandbox, task goals, user config and runtime tool policy. - Closure path: allow/deny a shell or file operation, nudge/stop a repeatedly failing agent and return technical status; no higher-purpose identity decision or constitutional binding return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: safety policy enforcement does not assume ownership of the organization&#39;s ultimate identity or mission. - Evidence: permissionmanager.rs; tools.rs; watchdog.rs; mod.rs; mod.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/dcee04ec283e9c0cc14102fea8ce86b587e3203a/assessments/zap.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit dcee04ec283e9c0cc14102fea8ce86b587e3203a\">dcee04e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-19T23:49:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Active Graph</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-19\">2026-10-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/tree/50d8405923b79e4e823a73a3045bb969436a872c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 50d8405923b79e4e823a73a3045bb969436a872c\">50d8405 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute context-sensitive agent steps against a changing graph/goal, using model-selected responses, tools and observed results in the supported live-provider mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New goal types, variable evidence and graph events, differing tool result content, tool/model failures and uncertain model next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Registered @llmbehavior provider model selects a response or declared tool call from the current graph view, tool definitions and prior tool messages; output handler turns model results into task-relevant graph mutations and reactive follow-up. - Decision owner: Configured LLM through first-party LLMBehavior, Runtime.invokellm, LLMProvider.complete and tool dispatch; the provider infrastructure is external, but the shipped model-decision composition is implemented here. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Event subscriptions, contextual graph view, frame, policy, tools registry, behavior scheduling, accepted patches, recorded event causality, tool-turn bounds and tool-error feedback. - Closure path: Goal/event → model-backed behavior → provider completion → selected tool(s) executed → result messages re-enter same model loop → final interpreted output and proposed graph patch → event-sourced graph updated → next event triggers model/deterministic behavior. - Boundary reachability: Public @llmbehavior and Runtime(graph,llmprovider=...) directly enter runtime-owned invokellm → live provider call/replay → tool dispatch → result to subsequent LLM turn → BehaviorGraph proposal. The bundled Diligence pack includes executable model-backed behavior definitions; the offline quickstart exercises identical event/behavior composition with a deliberately recorded surrogate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: If the model actor is removed but the event router, graph store, tool registry and policy remain, deterministic subscribers can fire but cannot generate varied task-sensitive decisions and tools from unknown evidence. Recorded fixtures are not live cognition; opt-in shipped provider mode is the S1 positive. - Evidence: activegraph/behaviors/decorators.py#L215, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1050, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1293, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1481, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1903, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1979, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L2097, activegraph/packs/diligence/behaviors.py#L111. - Basis: Structural first-party model-provider/tool feedback route with supported optional live mode, plus actual recorded-fixture offline quickstart. - Confidence: High for source reachability and offline execution; model service reliability unverified. - Caveats: Optional external provider SDK/API is needed for real LLM decisions; offline fixture success verifies the orchestrating mechanics only, not model performance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct first-party owner of regulation of specific disturbances between independently viable S1 operating divisions with mutually revised commitments is evidenced at the declared generic graph-runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent graph patches, relation-edge changes, conflicting object updates and event-trigger order can interfere with graph consistency; those disturbances are addressed by optimistic consistency/rejection and queued deterministic handling, not an evidenced portfolio of separately viable S1 operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Registered relation behaviors and graph-event selectors apply user-authored transformations; runtime queues, patch conflict rejection and serialized updates resolve graph state validity. No source-backed independent S1 units plus separate feedback-regulating owner are specified. - Decision owner: Developer-defined relation/regular behavior code and deterministic runtime patch/queue rules; any optional model-backed relation would need concrete independent-S1 ownership evidence beyond this general API. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed relations and reactive handlers, event subscription/patterns, optimistic patch validation, deterministic event queue, bounded scheduling and runtime commit log. - Closure path: Event/patch contention → deterministic commit/reject → subsequent event handlers read revised graph; this is shared-state coherence, not inter-S1 operational interference → S2 decision → adjusted independent organizational commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing a hypothetical coordinating model still leaves the same generic queue/patch-rejection machinery. A programmable relation handler is a possible application construction site, not a proven default S2 autonomously exercising relational coordination. - Evidence: activegraph/behaviors/decorators.py#L150, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1072, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1293, activegraph/runtime/authority.py#L103, activegraph/core/patch.py#L1, activegraph/core/graph.py#L1. - Basis: Bounded negative in first-party runtime and bundled example, not universal claim about all third-party packs. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A separate application may configure real independent S1 divisions and bind a model-led relation coordinator, requiring a new system boundary and evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party autonomous whole-current manager controls independent viable operating divisions&#39; resources, priorities and obligations with a managerial return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Event queue or LLM/tool budget exhaustion, failed behaviors, sandbox constraints and approval waiting can threaten a graph run, but these are local run-level risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer supplies Frame, Policy, budget limits, tools and registered behaviors; first-party executor checks them, schedules ready behaviors and emits failure/budget events. No separate agent is shown renegotiating a portfolio of independent S1 commitments. - Decision owner: Human/application constructor and deterministic runtime budget/scheduler/authority evaluator, not an operational S3 executive. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Frame goal/constraints, budget consumption and runquantum, scheduler, policy capability ceilings, approval and failure events, event sink status. - Closure path: Run condition → hard-coded/configured guard or scheduling choice → current behavior invoked/stopped/failure logged; missing whole-current viability manager → new binding priorities/resource rights returned to multiple S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Budget guards and queues are safety/throughput controls; removing a hypothetical model S3 still leaves all their fixed effects intact. - Evidence: activegraph/frame.py#L1, activegraph/policy.py#L1, activegraph/runtime/authority.py#L55, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1072, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L1364. - Basis: Bounded first-party negative including provider-configured model behavior, deterministic and pack modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Downstream application may manage many viable S1 systems atop this runtime; generic registered behavior count is not itself that scope.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate first-party complementary reality-inspection function independently challenges a current S3 manager&#39;s operating picture and returns a corrective action to that manager. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incomplete causal traces, untrusted tool/model outputs and behavior faults can mislead users. Event logs, strict replay, fork/diff and observability expose inconsistencies but do not themselves close managerial audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Replay divergence checks and error events can flag mismatched execution; an operator chooses what to inspect. No distinct S3 agent samples actual S1 reality, judges S3 reports and sends an authorized correction into separate S3 decision rights. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic trace/replay machinery and outside human/developer for interpretation; no autonomous audit owner at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Append-only event log, causal provenance, strict replay, tracing/metrics/sinks, patch rejection events and fork/diff. - Closure path: Events → trace or replay discrepancy → user inspection/failure event; no independent operational audit finding → established S3 receives it → changes current S1 portfolio control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Full replayability is valuable evidence infrastructure but removing a putative auditor does not change the deterministic event-history artifact. Evidence capture is not an independent audit decision/closure by itself. - Evidence: activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L3393, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L3583, activegraph/runtime/diff.py#L108, activegraph/trace/causal.py#L1, activegraph/observability/status.py#L1. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking dedicated observability and strict replay capabilities. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Users may implement a qualified external reviewer or application-specific reality-checking model; no such legitimate S3-to-S3 relation is bundled.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party model-owned future environment distinction → prospective changed strategy option → authorized durable adoption back into later operations cycle is established by the shipped event-sourcing/replay/fork mechanisms. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future model/prompt/graph configuration hypotheses, possible failure recurrence and drift can be explored with forks; the choices of what to test and retain remain application/developer-owned. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Runtime.fork creates a branch from recorded history, diff compares structural outcomes, and replay cache preserves event/model responses. The caller determines alternative behaviors/policies and whether/how to deploy them; no packaged model evaluates environmental future shifts and autonomously updates durable operating capabilities. - Decision owner: Outside experimenter/developer for prospective configuration; deterministic runtime for producing comparable fork traces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Replay cache, fork IDs, Diff computation, consistent event roots, saved packs/behaviors and optional user-supplied configuration. - Closure path: Operator forks run → changes input configuration → replay and diff → operator interprets result; no internal model-generated prospective strategy with authorized adoption into next live baseline operating mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A future developer can use the instrumentation for an S4 experiment, but removing any supposed strategic model does not disable graph replay, fork or diff; no model-owned future option-generation-and-adoption path is first-party evidenced. - Evidence: activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L3393, activegraph/runtime/runtime.py#L3583, activegraph/runtime/diff.py#L79, activegraph/llm/cache.py#L1, README.md#L42. - Basis: Bounded negative even though deterministic experimentation infrastructure is strong. - Confidence: Medium-high at the declared generic runtime boundary. - Caveats: A user-created LLM behavior might intentionally analyze trends and update a future skill; that is application-specific S4, not shipped default first-party ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party legitimate ultimate identity/purpose authority deciding constitutional conflicts with a lasting policy return is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool capabilities, graph mutation scope, replay security, sandbox access and human approvals bound model behavior but do not define organization-level identity decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Policy/frame ceilings and evaluateactionauthority enforce user-supplied permissions; explicit operator approval can control capabilities, but no internally accountable actor deliberates and sets final purpose for a viable organization. - Decision owner: External developer/operator supplies governance intent; first-party deterministic permission/approval enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AuthorityDecision, capability ceilings and policy configuration, patch validation, approval events, sandbox and budget locks. - Closure path: Tool/patch request → predetermined policy or outside approval → action allowed/denied; no identity-question escalation to legitimate S5 and durable return of constitutional policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The string Policy describes an enforceable rule set, not organizational legitimacy or the right to decide ultimate purpose. - Evidence: activegraph/policy.py#L1, activegraph/runtime/authority.py#L55, activegraph/runtime/authority.py#L103, activegraph/frame.py#L1, README.md#L150. - Basis: Bounded negative after first-party frame/authority and human approval modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The human/application owning a higher-level organization may exercise S5 externally; not claimed inside this runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3f3f65314f63d2b23f5de0b1c2512d780daee008/assessments/ac/active-graph.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3f3f65314f63d2b23f5de0b1c2512d780daee008\">3f3f653 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-10T17:45:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Hash-7777/HashCortX\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HashCortX</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-10\">2026-10-10</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Hash-7777/HashCortX/tree/ab3fb74a61928dfb98985eac5ec3eeac8c85afe7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ab3fb74a61928dfb98985eac5ec3eeac8c85afe7\">ab3fb74 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A single autonomous model-directed coding loop acts on user project files and tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing code, failing commands, requested edits and tool errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select files/commands/edits, observe first-party tool results and determine the next coding action. - Decision owner: Main model-driven HashCoder agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Native Tauri/Rust tool adapters, permission gates, model routing, session persistence, project scope and diff/undo. - Closure path: User task -&gt; model-chosen coding tool -&gt; first-party handler and tool feedback -&gt; new model choice or final result. - Boundary reachability: HashCoder&#39;s shipped code mode calls its native agentLoop and Tauri tool definitions on a real opened project. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses actions; the program executes and records those choices. - Evidence: mode.js; hashcoder.js; agent-policy.js - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Inference comes from configured external models; permissions and human approval can constrain actions..\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No established first-party inter-S1 regulation inside the HashCoder coding runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A single coding agent and its tool batch do not form distinct operational units in conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No S2-specific choice. - Decision owner: No S2 decision owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Concurrent read batching, sequential writes, session and UI state. - Closure path: Tools are grouped and executed for the same operational agent, not corrective coordination among independent S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parallel read tools and sequential writes only implement one agent&#39;s tool ordering; the separate Swarm workspace is excluded. - Evidence: agent-policy.js; mode.js; graph.js; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Sibling Swarm team design is an adjacent system and cannot donate its decisions to HashCoder..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct whole-system current management over multiple coding operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Iteration stalls, limits and tool permission issues are local operating disturbances. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None for organizational resource/commitment authority above one coding S1. - Decision owner: No qualifying S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Stall detector, step budgets, runtime gate, provider fallback, status and trace. - Closure path: Stalled or unproven work is returned to the same coding agent or stopped; no whole-current cross-unit regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic current action limits are enforcement mechanisms, not a metasystem choosing shared unit priorities. - Evidence: agent-policy.js; mode.js; verify.js - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: These protections may improve performance/safety but do not establish organizational S3..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent audit role with complementary evidence access and corrective organizational return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coding agent may prematurely claim correct changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Proof tracker evaluates recorded test commands and deterministic send-back conditions, not independent review judgment. - Decision owner: Main agent retains correction ownership; proof checker applies static record checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project test command discovery, proofLog, change timestamps and bounded completion prompts. - Closure path: Recorded same-loop tool output -&gt; deterministic nudge -&gt; same model executes or answers; no separate auditor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The evidence is generated from ordinary agent operations; separate audit access/owner is missing. - Evidence: verify.js; mode.js; agent-policy.js - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Proof log can honestly describe which tests ran, without becoming S3..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective external-environment adaptation loop in the credited HashCoder mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future capability demands and external changes are not explicitly converted into adaptation options. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No evidenced S4 capability renewal right. - Decision owner: No S4 agent, constructor or parent owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Automatic user-fact extraction, memory recall, model choice, persisted state and tools. - Closure path: Facts may affect later prompts but no prospective environmental intelligence decision changes the current operating organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context retention and provider fallback are not prospective organizational adaptation. - Evidence: store.js; mode.js; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Other desktop workspaces are separately scoped and cannot close S4 here..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party organizational identity or ultimate-policy governance loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool permission/safety choices concern individual operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No ultimate-policy decision right at the selected recursion. - Decision owner: Human grants individual permissions; deterministic rules enforce. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission Guard, Rust deny list, user action dialog, diff Keep/Undo, audit log. - Closure path: User approves or denies an operation, without a binding identity/ultimate-policy decision loop returned to the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Command authorization and selected model are not S5-level ultimate purpose. - Evidence: hashcoder.js; agentsandbox.rs; SECURITY.md - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Runtime control is not inherited from broader OSS development governance..\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4ed17d3bae037310dcd91af45f247ffb34cbe94e/assessments/hashcortx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4ed17d3bae037310dcd91af45f247ffb34cbe94e\">4ed17d3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T03:03:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nattergabriel/termcoder\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Termcoder</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nattergabriel/termcoder/tree/c642c36e8d65ca42a8ae05172c527f73cb2017d7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c642c36e8d65ca42a8ae05172c527f73cb2017d7\">c642c36 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry out uncertain coding/developer tasks through chosen code-inspection, search, editing and shell operations informed by real tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown project structure, unavailable files, code defects, command failure, unexpected results and subsequent needed steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses a sequence of typed tools and the follow-up or terminal response based on previous tool outputs. - Decision owner: model-driven first-party Agent.runturn workflow. Provider supplies model inference but the installed app binds tools, executes actions and supplies feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Registry, provider adapter, State, tool implementations, permission callbacks, channel UI, config/skills, iteration cap and rollback. - Closure path: user task → model tool request → permission decision → real registered tool execution → ToolResult appended as model context → model chooses next action or completes turn. - Boundary reachability: app.build supplies the native registry/provider/permission callback to Agent, and the supported terminal/Telegram modes run that same first-party loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model choice leaves a deterministic tool list and permission prompts but no context-sensitive coding plan, inspection or completion decision. - Evidence: agent loop; app composition; tools; integration tests. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high for implementation-defined tool feedback; live provider-backed task not reproduced. - Caveats: mutating calls remain subject to human permission, and external providers remain dependencies.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no autonomous or constructor-owned first-party regulation of specific interference/oscillation between distinct operating S1 units is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple tool calls and incoming prompts are handled within one agent&#39;s own sequence, rather than competing independently goal-directed agent workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: serial dispatchall runs model-requested tools; UI input queue transports future user messages without conflict arbitration between autonomous S1s. - Decision owner: model for its own operation, runtime for serial dispatch, channel for input handling; no separate S2 closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, sequential dispatch, Telegram input queue, slash-command/user channel routing. - Closure path: result of each tool feeds the same model&#39;s next inference. No inter-S1 conflict signal → specific attenuation decision → changed subsequent worker obligations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sequencing one operative&#39;s tools or swapping communications channels is not the S2 functional relation. - Evidence: loop.py; telegram.py; app.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within shipped single-agent modes. - Caveats: independent user-launched termcoder processes could interfere externally without creating native cross-process coordination.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-current management function over a population of operating S1 units with authority over shared resources/commitments is instantiated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current task and conversation progress are local to the only S1, not a managed organization&#39;s distinct current commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coding model decides its own tool sequence; channel/user can approve or refuse operations; no whole-system manager allocates resources, changes priority across workers or returns corrected commitments. - Decision owner: the active coding model, human permissions/configuration and deterministic runtime; no separate S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: iteration limit, provider/model commands, task-local message state, approval gate. - Closure path: task feedback and user guidance directly return to the operative model; no distinct whole-current view → corrective S3 decision → multiple S1 adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: present coding-task management is S1 even when the model can change models or adjust its next action. - Evidence: app.py; loop.py; model command; config. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for standard application boundary. - Caveats: external human operating several instances is not first-party returned S3 governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent reality-sampling auditor emits a separate judgment and closes a feedback path into S3 management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool/test failures can be seen by the active coding model and tests can be run as tools, but these are ordinary execution feedback rather than independent operational auditing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model may invoke tests/shell or view error messages; repo integration tests run by external developers/CI. None constitutes a first-party independent audit decision and management return. - Decision owner: operative coding model for tool-followup, developer/CI for separate quality tests; no S3 audit owner in the deployed agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shell result, tool error marker, iterative model reasoning, developer tests. - Closure path: failed tool or test output → same agent&#39;s subsequent action; no independent complementary auditor → managerial owner → binding corrective instruction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: routine self-verification and off-runtime test suites do not establish the S3 function. - Evidence: loop.py; Bash tool; loop test; development CI. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: human review or a downstream plugin could introduce an audit, but no such shipped ownership loop was found.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party closed future/environment-sensing adaptation function is shipped to alter sustained organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing provider/models and source content may influence today&#39;s coding task; there is no prospective adaptation program. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user selects/configures provider, model and skills; a model may adapt local tactics after tool feedback, not authorize a future-oriented change in organizational capability. - Decision owner: user/developer for configuration and skill provision, current task model for immediate work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider registry, slash commands, global/project config, discovered Agent Skills and project instructions. - Closure path: user changes settings → next local session/tool choices; no distinct outside/future observation → capability proposal → S4 decision → returned organizational adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-local reasoning, model switching and skill activation are not substantive external-and-prospective S4 by themselves. - Evidence: config.py; app.py; SkillCatalog; provider command. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high on bounded standard modes. - Caveats: custom extensions might implement a separate adaptation function, not attributed to standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party legitimate ultimate-policy or continuing-identity arbitration loop at the agent-organization boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission postures, project instructions and credentials govern local tool execution, not organization identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human approval and static policy choose whether particular tool calls are allowed. No S5 identity decision is made and returned as binding whole-system governance. - Decision owner: user/configuration for permissions, deterministic runtime for enforcement; no S5 decisive owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: askeach, allowreadonly, allowall, project/user config and tool metadata. - Closure path: user/local config → permission callback → tool allowed/denied. No legitimate ultimate authority receives an S5 issue and returns an organizational identity-level decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a permission gate or a system prompt cannot be credited as ultimate policy/identity decision ownership without the function-specific closure. - Evidence: permissions.py; config.py; app.py; prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within shipped modes. - Caveats: project maintainer governance occurs outside the packaged agent&#39;s operational recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e953e47c78813c4c7796ae402f875b38e866a890/assessments/termcoder.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e953e47c78813c4c7796ae402f875b38e866a890\">e953e47 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-24T19:38:32Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Airavat-Research/cascaide-ts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cascaide-ts</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Airavat-Research/cascaide-ts/tree/08fc10fcd58ecf729b9b0c5c1f0c135f7bb723f7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 08fc10fcd58ecf729b9b0c5c1f0c135f7bb723f7\">08fc10f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform goal-directed agent work through repeated model decisions, tool/delegation actions and returned observations within a Cascaide cascade. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user/application requests, evolving conversation history, model uncertainty, tool/sub-agent outputs and errors, provider responses and the local state needed to decide whether to act again or complete. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive answer, direct tool call, self-delegation or named-sub-agent delegation from the current model-visible context and incorporate returned results into the next turn. - Decision owner: the model-driven agent actor invoked through the first-party helper-built agent node. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: core node lifecycle, workflow context, provider adapters, tool-node execution, child-cascade spawning/waiting, recursion depth guard, streaming normalization, active-node tracking, optional persistence/hydration and client/server relay. - Closure path: cascade input/history → helper agent node → provider-backed model decision → answer or Tool/delegation call → first-party tool node executes the selected action/subcascade → tool result is appended to cascade history → helper respawns the agent node → model receives the observation and decides again or terminates. - Boundary reachability: @cascaide-ts/helpers is a shipped package explicitly documented as providing createReactAgent, createRecursiveReactAgent, createSupervisorAgent and createRecursiveSupervisorAgent; those generated nodes execute directly through the documented @cascaide-ts/core engine rather than through a repository-development example path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Cascaide&#39;s deterministic graph executor transports, persists and bounds the loop, while the model actor chooses substantive next actions from the current history and declared capabilities. External providers supply inference but are directly invoked as the actor in the first-party operating path. - Evidence: createAgent.ts; serverWorkflowListenerMiddleware.ts; packages/helpers/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the application chooses the model, system prompt, direct tools and optional sub-agent catalog. Those constructor inputs bound S1 but do not move the turn-level action decision out of the model actor once the standard helper runtime is executing.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination function is established at the declared cascade recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel or delegated S1 units could in principle overlap, conflict or oscillate, but the reviewed standard distribution does not identify a concrete sibling-interference mode and close a response specifically intended to damp it. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegateToSubAgents() can spawn several child cascades and wait for them; recursive delegation is described for independent subtasks; activeNodes tracks concurrent work; persistence claims prevent duplicate execution of a node instance. - Closure path: no first-party disturbance-specific coordination judgment was found that returns a conflict/oscillation attenuation decision into subsequent behavior of multiple S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model may choose subtasks or named sub-agents, but delegation and task decomposition are not S2 by themselves. spawn, waitUntil, active-node bookkeeping and node claiming transport/order execution without selecting a coordination response to a evidenced inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: createAgent.ts; controller.ts; serverPersistenceMiddleware.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream graph/node code could use the generic controller to implement a domain-specific coordination mechanism, but a universal extension surface is not itself an S2 constructor path without function-specific interference and closure evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-cascade current-control function with agent-owned authority is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current whole-cascade commitments, competing priorities, resource constraints or local-vs-global operational exceptions would require a metasystemic current view and intervention right, but the packaged agent paths do not provide that relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established over current resources, commitments, priorities or constraints for the cascade as a whole. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkflowState exposes context/active nodes/history/errors; CascadeController can read state, spawn nodes, wait and fork; the Supervisor model can delegate to configured sub-agents and receives their completed outputs as tool results; execution-time buffering can checkpoint remaining graph work. - Closure path: no packaged path was found in which an agent receives a whole-cascade current operational view, chooses a current-control intervention over the organization as a whole and returns that decision into runtime state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Supervisor delegation remains task allocation. The standard Supervisor receives completed child results, not a first-party live whole-system current-control view. Generic controller state/read/write methods are handed to node/tool implementation code and can support downstream specialization, but they do not define or own an S3-specific decision right. - Evidence: createAgent.ts; controller.ts; workflowSlice.ts; serverWorkflowListenerMiddleware.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an application may deliberately expose controller state and intervention tools to a model or human manager, but that would be a separate downstream organization requiring its own evidence rather than an inherited S3 state for the reusable base runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operational claims could require evidence beyond ordinary agent output/history, but the standard runtime does not add an independent challenge path over those claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for independent audit judgment. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable context events, node history/errors, full audit trails described for durable mode, provider-stream canonicalization, cascade hydration and fork/time-travel. - Closure path: no first-party path was found from complementary evidence or independent challenge → audit finding → downstream corrective current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistence and replay improve observability and reconstructability, but they record the ordinary execution path. Forking a prior state permits alternative execution but supplies no independent auditor, distinct evidence channel or corrective audit authority by itself. - Evidence: serverPersistenceMiddleware.ts; serverHydrationMiddleware.ts; persistence.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream applications can build reviewers or validators as ordinary graph nodes, but no packaged independent audit relation with complementary access and corrective closure is owned by the reusable runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-future adaptation function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external conditions, opportunities, threats or future capability needs are not modeled by a packaged prospective adaptation loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for generating and selecting future-oriented adaptation options from external distinctions. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: cascade history/context persistence, hydration, forking/time travel, recursive delegation and application-definable graph transitions. - Closure path: no first-party external/future sensing → adaptation-option generation → return into current capability/control path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: durable state and forkable histories concern preservation/replay of current or past execution. Recursive delegation and graph evolution during a task remain operational/current regulation unless evidence establishes prospective environmental modeling and capability adaptation. - Evidence: serverHydrationMiddleware.ts; controller.ts; workflowSlice.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the generic graph can host research/adaptation agents supplied by an application, but those are downstream semantics rather than a first-party S4 constructor relation in the reviewed base distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the cascade recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity-level conflicts or unresolved S3–S4 policy tensions have no packaged escalation/decision path in the reusable runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for legitimate ultimate-policy or identity decisions. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: application-provided systemPrompt, model/tool/sub-agent configuration, UI-node/HITL construction capability, provider filters and generic graph controls. - Closure path: no identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → decision → subsequent-operation governance path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts and configuration constrain behavior but do not themselves exercise runtime identity authority. Human interaction can be implemented as a client/UI node, including in scaffolded applications, but generic task-level HITL does not establish S5 and the reusable base does not define an ultimate-policy escalation relation. - Evidence: packages/helpers/src/agent/types.ts; clientWorkflowListenerMiddleware.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream application may define identity/policy-specific human or agent nodes. Such a specialization is a separate system-in-focus and cannot donate S5 to the reusable Cascaide constructor/runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1a87050bf4e179a45f42eaca88d2515082fdac2a/assessments/cascaide-ts.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1a87050bf4e179a45f42eaca88d2515082fdac2a\">1a87050 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
